WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.284 --> 00:00:11.680
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, welcome everybody to the&nbsp;
public session of NHS England and NHS improvement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:20.080
I’ve got apologies from Dido and Ara. And&nbsp;
first I could just say that this is Noel’s last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:27.760
board meeting, and Noel, just on behalf of us&nbsp;
all, thank you very much for the huge contribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:35.840
you’ve made to the NHS over many years and also&nbsp;
for the time you’ve spent at NHS digital as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.383
which has been hugely appreciated by all of us.
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Thank you, David.&nbsp;

00:00:40.383 --> 00:00:46.880
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Noel. And, and secondly,&nbsp;
just to congratulate Tim on behalf of everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:53.360
again for your appointment, Tim as the national&nbsp;
director for transformation, beginning in May.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.440
Any declarations of interest that&nbsp;
aren’t or haven’t already been declared?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.040
Can I approve the minutes of the&nbsp;
last board meetings? Good. So Simon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:12.480
over to you for the chief executive’s update.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Okay, thank you very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:19.760
David and afternoon, colleagues. Firstly,&nbsp;
can I note the sad death of Dr Cliff Mann,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:26.960
who was our national clinical director for urgent&nbsp;
and emergency care. And Cliff was a great friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:33.360
and a source of wise advice to the whole of the&nbsp;
NHS, as well as remaining very closely grounded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:40.080
the realities of day-to-day emergency medicine.&nbsp;
So I think we have taken the opportunity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.760
mark the contribution that Cliff has made and&nbsp;
say to his family how sorry we are at his loss.&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.440
If I could also add my thanks to Noel&nbsp;
Gordon, as you have done David, for Noel’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:59.600
fantastic non-executive leadership on the NHS&nbsp;
England board, right back to 2014 in a variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:07.600
of capacities and other NHS service as well.
And then to set the scene or set the table for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.160
our public meeting this afternoon, three&nbsp;
points really just to get us kicked off.&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:20.320
Firstly, in terms of the current position in&nbsp;
respect of COVID and the pressures in the NHS, I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:26.720
pleased to say that the impatient numbers continue&nbsp;
to come down very sharply. You’ll remember that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:36.080
had over 34,000 severely ill coronavirus patients&nbsp;
in our hospitals in mid-January. That number is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:44.080
now 4,000. And although that is still&nbsp;
about 400 more COVID patients than we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:50.240
this same day a year ago, nevertheless, that&nbsp;
very sharp decrease in the number of patients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:56.960
with COVID in hospital is a consequence of both&nbsp;
declining infection rates across the community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.720
and the impact that’s now being&nbsp;
felt from the vaccination programme.&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.400
And as a result of those much reduced&nbsp;
acute pressures on the health service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.600
today, I’m recommending that we reduce the&nbsp;
national alert level across the health service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:21.360
the EPRR alert level, from level four to&nbsp;
level three, and that would take effect today.&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.480
And as I say, that is partly&nbsp;
as a consequence of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.000
second huge field of activity&nbsp;
that we’ve been involved in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.360
since our last public board meeting, which is the&nbsp;
continuing rollout of the vaccination programme.&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:44.160
Since our last public meeting on the 28th of&nbsp;
January, we have administered 19.5 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:51.120
COVID vaccinations across England. And&nbsp;
within the next 24 hours or so, we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:59.840
pass the 25 million mark, 25 million people across&nbsp;
England who’ve had at least their first jab.&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:06.880
We are, as we’ll hear from Emily Lawson in a&nbsp;
moment, completing the job for the first nine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:15.200
high risk groups, people aged 50 and above and&nbsp;
other high risk individuals, and we have for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.960
latest age group that we’ve been targeting, the 50&nbsp;
to 54 year olds, gone from a position where about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:26.160
two fifths of those people in their early 50s had&nbsp;
had their vaccination at the start of last week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.360
to a position where in the next 24 hours or so&nbsp;
we should be at three quarters of people having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:37.280
had their jabs. And that I might add includes&nbsp;
me and I was very grateful to be vaccinated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:46.000
last week, along with millions of others of my age&nbsp;
group. And so we are now particularly gearing up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:52.000
as we’ll hear from Emily, for second doses, with&nbsp;
April being a particular focus on second doses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.120
Two million people across England have already&nbsp;
had their second COVID vaccination and yesterday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.920
over 40% of the vaccinations that were&nbsp;
given out across England were second doses.&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.560
So, given the decreasing COVID pressures,&nbsp;
given the accelerating COVID vaccination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:18.000
the third thing to set the scene is that today&nbsp;
we set out the operating rhythm for the NHS for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:25.520
the year ahead for financial year 2021/22, which&nbsp;
begins on the first of April. And there’s been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:32.960
lot of work across our entire organisation. Some&nbsp;
of it behind the scenes working with the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:38.320
with our partners and patients groups&nbsp;
and with government to get this right.&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:44.880
Today the government have published the&nbsp;
mandate for the National Health Service for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.760
year ahead, and matching that this&nbsp;
afternoon we are discussing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:53.520
approving the operating planning guidance&nbsp;
for the health service for the year ahead.&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.280
We’ve got funding certainty for our COVID&nbsp;
costs for the first half of the year, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.480
I personally felt was very important that we had&nbsp;
that planning certainty. We’ve got extra funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:12.640
that we’ll be deploying to tackle long waiting&nbsp;
lists. We are doubling down on the investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:18.720
guarantees that we have made for mental health&nbsp;
services and GP and community services. And as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.120
we’ll hear later on this afternoon, additional&nbsp;
investment for maternity services as well.&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.280
And within the last week, we have formally&nbsp;
approved every part of the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:35.840
to be designated as integrated care systems,&nbsp;
showing the benefit that joined up working has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.720
brought over the course of the last year.
So David, those I think, are the three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:47.840
scene-setters for our discussions this afternoon,&nbsp;
on COVID, on vaccinations and on the year ahead.&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:55.120
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Simon, that’s&nbsp;
very clear. Would anyone like to kick it off? Ara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:03.360
you’ve got views, very strong views on vaccines,&nbsp;
and you’ve been involved with the NHS for many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.120
many years now. Where do you see the&nbsp;
biggest challenges for us as going forward?&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:15.280
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi of Denham: Well, I think, firstly, we&nbsp;
have to acknowledge the amazing work that the NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.880
England has done in getting the vaccines out. And&nbsp;
thanks to Emily, and many, many, the great team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:28.640
that Simon leads on. It’s really the pride of what&nbsp;
we’ve done in terms of tackling seeing the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:34.320
this. I mean, the vaccine, we were not just good&nbsp;
enough in the implementation, the whole pathway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:40.560
the procurement of it, the decision making, and&nbsp;
obviously, the jabs, you know, achieving 850,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:47.520
in one day, that’s a global record in terms of&nbsp;
per capita, that’s a phenomenal achievement.&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.480
Where are we going with the vaccine? I think&nbsp;
we’re, obviously there will be the cohort who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:58.080
needs to get their second jab. I think we’re&nbsp;
moving in the different categories. As we all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:05.040
know, it’s all in the public domain, I think&nbsp;
the next is how do we use the vaccine. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:12.800
was at a meeting this morning to see what type&nbsp;
of a certification we need in terms of, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:21.680
an individual citizen could free up and to, you&nbsp;
know, go back to work and start businesses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:28.080
contribute to the economy, obviously, with the&nbsp;
constraint still that we need to be careful not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.720
to get the balance right between the security&nbsp;
of the vaccine but at the same time that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:38.560
don’t lose some of the interventions that&nbsp;
we put in place like social distancing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:43.040
wearing masks. And I mean, from a vaccine&nbsp;
perspective, there are two elements from it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:48.400
do we – vaccine protects the individual, but&nbsp;
at the same time, we need to ensure that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:55.840
individual is not transmitting virus to others.
So that still remains a gap in science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:01.600
which we’re looking at at the moment.&nbsp;
There are obviously a, you know, when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:08.880
how we integrate COVID vaccinations as part&nbsp;
of our annual flu vaccines in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:13.520
but all positive, you know, something&nbsp;
that this nation should be very proud of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:19.920
and the taxpayer should be very proud&nbsp;
of the NHS. A remarkable job, really.&nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:25.600
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ara. And Laura, I’m&nbsp;
looking at Laura Wade-Gery, in terms of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:30.480
digital changes that have happened over the last&nbsp;
year, I mean, it’s been an extraordinary change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:37.920
I think over the last 12 months.
&gt;&gt;Laura Wade-Gery: Yes. Thank you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:50.320
David. Indeed. I think that the progress that&nbsp;
the NHS has made in moving to digital services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:57.120
has been truly, it has taken, I think, almost the&nbsp;
service itself by surprise in terms of the change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:04.320
that has been possible, and the extent that that&nbsp;
has actually enabled normal service to continue.&nbsp;

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:08.240
So whether that’s the rollout of&nbsp;
attend anywhere to enable outpatients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:14.800
or indeed much more use of online triage&nbsp;
with GPs, I think it’s been an amazing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:23.040
amazing year that we now have the opportunity&nbsp;
to build on as we go into the work that we’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:28.640
talk about today in terms of the recovery&nbsp;
programme, and putting, increasingly putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:35.600
digital at the heart of all of our activity.
So I’d like to kind of congratulate I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:44.000
teams in NHS Digital and NHS X as well as E for&nbsp;
kind of the leadership they’ve shown but actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:50.640
everybody at the frontline for how much they&nbsp;
have adopted and kind of taken forward what’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.040
been made available or they have found.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:59.680
Thanks, Laura. And Andrew Morris, I mean,&nbsp;
you’ve been in the NHS for 40 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:04.720
and you’ve seen it through good times and bad&nbsp;
times or tough times and less tough times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:09.040
or maybe all the times are pretty tough. I mean,&nbsp;
how would you characterise today’s challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:13.280
compared to what you’ve seen in the past?
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: So I think the last 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:20.400
months have been the toughest of the tough. Going&nbsp;
forward, getting the backlog on the waiting list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:26.560
down is going to be the one priority. But we’ve&nbsp;
got to look after our staff first and foremost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:33.280
They’ve gone through a really challenging time.&nbsp;
So I think all the efforts that people have made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:40.000
we’ve got to recognise and I think employers,&nbsp;
trusts that is, have got to do all the general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:46.400
kind of housekeeping bits, making sure that&nbsp;
people take the leave that’s due, looking after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:53.040
you know, the health and wellbeing of their&nbsp;
staff, and giving people an opportunity to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.240
spend time with their family and&nbsp;
not focus on the NHS for once.&nbsp;

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:03.440
However, we’ve still got the big job of&nbsp;
bringing waiting times down, which will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:09.360
be probably the biggest challenge for the next&nbsp;
two years. And everyone needs to be, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:14.800
focused on that. And I’m sure clinicians want&nbsp;
to get back to working in a routine way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:20.080
they’ve done before. I think there are some&nbsp;
fantastic opportunities around transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:26.320
doing things differently, and using the ICSs,&nbsp;
the integrated care systems, as a way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:33.360
improving pathways and taking&nbsp;
steps out of pathways so we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:39.600
get a faster slicker service to all.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Andrew. And David Behan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:44.640
just on the workforce point that Andrew raised&nbsp;
there. I mean, in a sense, one can never do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:52.000
enough for the people who have done so much over&nbsp;
the last 12 months. But what’s your perceptions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:57.360
where the workforce are at the moment?
&gt;&gt;David Behan: So just to reflect comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:03.760
that colleagues have made about the emotional&nbsp;
and psychological and physical demands that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:11.520
have been made on staff. But I think to Simon’s&nbsp;
introduction, looking to the future, I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:17.520
planning guidance for this year is really clear&nbsp;
on the importance of supporting staff, and we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:23.920
to do that in the short term. Investing in our&nbsp;
staff is one way to ensure that the point that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:29.120
Andrew’s just made about working on the backlog&nbsp;
and doing that quickly, is going to be secured.&nbsp;

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:35.200
One of the things that’s impressed me, though,&nbsp;
about staff during the past 12 months, David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:42.400
has been their agility, their ability to&nbsp;
work flexibly, the ability to work in teams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:48.960
and I think that’s a fantastic reflection on&nbsp;
staff. And it’s important we secure some of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:54.160
changes as we look to the future and go forward.
From an education and training point of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.720 --> 00:14:00.560
it’s absolutely critical we begin to secure a&nbsp;
pipeline of people coming through. The brilliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:08.400
news is that the past 12 months has served as a&nbsp;
real advert for health and care professionals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:14.800
applications to university for both nursing and&nbsp;
medicine degrees are up quite significantly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:18.560
They were up last year and they were up again in&nbsp;
terms of applications to university this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:26.160
which is a fantastic development. And I think that&nbsp;
can begin to give us some confidence that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:33.040
secure the future workforce. But we do need a long&nbsp;
term, multi-year settlement for the workforce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:38.640
because we need to plan this over the long&nbsp;
term and think not just about 21/22 but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:43.680
next 10 years or so where we can actually lock&nbsp;
in some of the changes, the transformational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:48.000
changes that we’ve seen. And I think to&nbsp;
Laura’s point, digital is going to be a key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:53.680
enabler to that. So investing in the workforce&nbsp;
and investing digitally is key to the future.&nbsp;

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:58.000
But tremendous achievements over the past&nbsp;
12 years, over the past 12 months, sorry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.520
and the planning guidance, I think&nbsp;
gives some real clarity on what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:07.440
the next steps are for the system. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, David. So Simon, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:14.880
if you think back to before the coming together of&nbsp;
NHS E and NHS – NHS England and NHS Improvement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:21.040
I think the, we now have a sort of a unified&nbsp;
team at the centre, which I think has made a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:27.440
difference. And so I think from all the non-exec&nbsp;
directors of NHS England and NHS Improvement, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:36.800
know, a huge thank you to the executive team who&nbsp;
have put in so much time and energy and intellect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:43.280
into this over the over the last 12 months. So&nbsp;
thank you very much for that to the whole team.&nbsp;

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:47.680
So, shall we move on to Emily then,&nbsp;
Simon, or did you have anything else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:50.960
you wanted to raise or should we go on&nbsp;
to the vaccine deployment programme?&nbsp;

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:54.480
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yeah, let’s go on to Emily.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay, Emily.&nbsp;

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:01.120
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Thank you chair. So,&nbsp;
I thought I’d give just a few words of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:05.040
introduction given that we’ve already discussed&nbsp;
some of the aspects of the vaccination programme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:12.960
and the paper is presented for the board to note.
So the news as discussed is that the vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:17.920
deployment programme continues to go well, so&nbsp;
we met our mid-February target of offering to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:24.960
cohorts one to four and we are on track to meet&nbsp;
the target for mid-April of offering to cohorts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:30.720
one to nine, indeed, all of cohorts one to nine&nbsp;
currently have the option of being vaccinated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:35.360
and we’re seeing tremendous uptake,&nbsp;
as the board has already noted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:40.320
particularly last week, where over 3 million&nbsp;
vaccinations were delivered in week for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:45.440
first time and second and first doses being&nbsp;
delivered at reasonable scale in the same week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:50.320
with a step up in second doses already&nbsp;
this week as we track over the week of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:55.840
the fourth of January, where 1.5 million&nbsp;
vaccines were delivered in the first place.&nbsp;

00:16:55.840 --> 00:17:01.680
So those second doses will track up and April&nbsp;
will be, as Simon mentioned, second dose month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:08.560
with over 2.5 million second doses&nbsp;
being delivered in most of those weeks.&nbsp;

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:14.000
So we’ve made significant progress on&nbsp;
those, on that delivery of first doses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:18.400
and now need to make sure we complete&nbsp;
people’s vaccinations with the second doses.&nbsp;

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:25.120
The vaccination’s been delivered through primary&nbsp;
care, GPs have received their notifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:29.360
of second dose deliveries and when they&nbsp;
will come through to second half of April,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.960
and we’ll get those through to the end of May&nbsp;
shortly or the middle of May shortly so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:39.040
they can book appointments accordingly.
And listening to feedback from sites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:43.200
trying to stick to single days for single sites&nbsp;
so that they know what’s coming and when and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:46.560
can book appointments, particularly&nbsp;
for second doses, far enough ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:52.080
to give everybody confidence in that process.
For the next few weeks, there will be a limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:58.160
number of first doses delivered. So we will be&nbsp;
working really closely with sites, local systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:04.640
and regions to make sure that those are routed to&nbsp;
communities and systems which have lower uptake in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:09.680
cohorts one to nine, to ensure that everyone in&nbsp;
cohorts one to nine has both received an offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:16.720
of a vaccine and an opportunity to take it up.
Efforts on uptake have been a core part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:22.560
team’s activity over the last, well, over the&nbsp;
whole period, but particularly over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:29.520
sort of eight to nine weeks as we’ve got better&nbsp;
data on uptake and where uptake is, where uptake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:33.920
is going faster than in other places.
Critically following the World Health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:39.680
Organization’s guidance on what it takes to&nbsp;
achieve outstanding uptake and to ensure everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:45.360
feels comfortable to come forward to receive their&nbsp;
vaccine. This is as far as possible community led,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:52.480
we’ve provided funding via CCGs to work with&nbsp;
local communities to identify ways in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:57.680
additional sites, for example pop up sites,&nbsp;
can help people feel comfortable to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:02.400
forward and access the vaccine when that&nbsp;
offer is made to them. And we’re continuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:07.920
to innovate working with local communities.
And we’ve started to see the real impact of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:13.520
by tracking those who come forward and the&nbsp;
penetration of vaccine uptake in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:21.840
communities. So if I give you one example, those&nbsp;
who are, who’ve identified their ethnicity as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:28.400
either Asian or Asian British with a Bangladeshi&nbsp;
origin, where there’s been efforts led by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:35.200
Bangladeshi community leaders, by Imams, and by&nbsp;
local communities, including Na’eem Ahmed from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:43.120
people team, we saw a change in the uptake of&nbsp;
the vaccine within all of cohorts one to nine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:49.680
between the first of February where 15% of that&nbsp;
group had had the vaccine, bearing in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:52.560
of course, that not everybody in one to&nbsp;
nine had been offered the chance to have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:59.520
vaccine at that point, to over 75% yesterday.
So a real acceleration of uptake in a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:03.440
where we’ve identified what particularly&nbsp;
works for that community, what sites work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:08.480
the use of pop-ups, religious and other&nbsp;
community leaders to help increase confidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:15.040
decrease complacency in the vaccine, and make sure&nbsp;
people feel comfortable accepting their offer.&nbsp;

00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:19.920
So the work for the next few weeks will obviously&nbsp;
focus on outstanding operational delivery every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:24.000
day to give people confidence that they’ll&nbsp;
receive their second dose, that the vaccine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:28.400
that they should take up their offer of the&nbsp;
vaccine when it’s available to them, make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:33.520
sites have what they need to deliver every day,&nbsp;
and to continue to work with communities to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:37.840
sure we’re innovating and working with local&nbsp;
groups to make sure everybody feels confident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:44.800
having the vaccine when it’s their turn. Over.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:49.360
Do put your hands up, anyone who wants to raise&nbsp;
any questions from the board. And we’re looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:55.440
at looking at the programme through the lens&nbsp;
of health inequalities, an issue that Wol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.440 --> 00:21:01.360
has raised a number of times in the past. I mean&nbsp;
how, you gave a particular example, but overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:09.520
are you, not happy with it, are you pleased&nbsp;
with the degree of uptake from particularly from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:15.600
Black and minority ethnic communities?
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.600 --> 00:21:21.680
I think you chose your words carefully, David.&nbsp;
If I would, if I can give the sort of dimensions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:28.400
of that, that the uptake from Black and minority&nbsp;
ethnic groups of the vaccine is way in advance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:33.840
any other public health campaign of the last&nbsp;
several years. And you know, in some groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:38.880
for example, it is double the uptake for flu.&nbsp;
So there’s been something about the pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:45.520
that’s really managed to catalyse that sense&nbsp;
of personal and collective responsibility.&nbsp;

00:21:46.640 --> 00:21:52.800
And what we’ve had with the transparency of&nbsp;
data that we’ve got is the ability to track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:58.000
daily the uptake both at a national level but&nbsp;
also locally, and therefore work with local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:02.000
communities to understand what’s getting in the&nbsp;
way, right? To take access out of the equation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:07.920
to work with local leaders to address confidence,&nbsp;
to make sure messages are being shared by those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:12.400
who are trusted to deliver the message, and&nbsp;
also to use this as an opportunity for to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:17.440
increase access to health care more broadly.&nbsp;
So we have several initiatives, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:21.360
of running, I mean, this doesn’t address&nbsp;
the Black and minority ethnic group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:29.440
but a group that also doesn’t access all of all&nbsp;
healthcare services in equal levels to those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:36.000
living in different environments, so the homeless&nbsp;
community, for example, local sites have run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:42.400
integrated health clinics, offering dentistry,&nbsp;
ophthalmology and vaccination at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:46.960
and encouraging people to register with a&nbsp;
GP who haven’t previously been registered.&nbsp;

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:51.200
So I think the programme’s given us, through&nbsp;
the example I cited earlier and that example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:55.120
the opportunity to, you know, increase&nbsp;
our level of trust and connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.120 --> 00:23:00.400
with the health service more broadly.
So while the uptake in Black and minority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.400 --> 00:23:07.120
ethnic groups doesn’t track in every part of the&nbsp;
country the same level as white British uptake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:12.880
for example, there is also an intersectionality&nbsp;
aspect in that those cohorts with the highest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:19.440
levels of deprivation have not come forward in&nbsp;
as high numbers as those in the least deprived.&nbsp;

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:24.800
While those numbers aren’t the same, I’m not&nbsp;
happy. But I think we have to recognise that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:31.360
this has already, this has in many ways in terms&nbsp;
of uptake gone incredibly well. We won’t rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:36.080
until every community feels that they’ve been&nbsp;
involved in that opportunity to come forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:41.200
that we’ve answered any concerns and questions,&nbsp;
not we necessarily, but the trusted leaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:45.120
clinical leaders, religious leaders have&nbsp;
answered and been able to have conversations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:49.840
about the concerns that people have.
And we see every day, every week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:55.280
that penetration or that uptake ticking up&nbsp;
gradually. And as I said, with, I picked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.280 --> 00:24:02.800
Bangladeshi community example, because that&nbsp;
showed such a steep increase in uptake when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:06.400
had the right combination of working with&nbsp;
local leaders, with pop up clinics and of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:11.360
the right messaging. So we need to find what&nbsp;
is that right combination for all communities.&nbsp;

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:16.320
And the level of ownership by individual&nbsp;
communities is tremendous. So we just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:20.000
need to make sure we keep that up and&nbsp;
we keep making sure everybody’s aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:24.880
that the offer is made at a certain point in&nbsp;
time, but it’s open forever. Right? If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:27.920
come forward now and you’re in cohort two&nbsp;
and didn’t take up the offer at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:32.800
we will find you a vaccination. And it’s&nbsp;
really important that I hope everybody hears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:37.680
that message. And everybody who isn’t confident&nbsp;
knows you know, they can have a conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:42.160
we’ll provide the right materials, the&nbsp;
right people, the right environment, I hope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:47.120
to deal with any concerns that people have.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Emily. So Wol, then Ian.&nbsp;

00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:53.120
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thanks, David. And Emily, thanks&nbsp;
for outlining your efforts in this area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:58.560
which seem to be going well. I think&nbsp;
though that the challenge continues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:03.520
and I know you’re not complacent, even remotely.
As we move out of cohorts one to nine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:10.240
the things that worked back in, you know,&nbsp;
that you’ve tried so far, may not work quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:14.080
so well as you move down the age group. So and&nbsp;
I know you’re on it, but I just want to really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:18.880
be absolutely doubly sure that you’re totally on&nbsp;
it. The combination of different things, the local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:22.640
leaders, religious leaders may not work quite&nbsp;
so well in this next phase. And just making sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.640 --> 00:25:25.920
we’re innovating and being agile in the way we’re&nbsp;
approaching this is really, really important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:30.960
That’s the first question, or comment.
The second question is around some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:34.400
the things you’re not in control of Emily, and&nbsp;
this may be something that some of our clinical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:38.080
colleagues here, I’m looking particularly&nbsp;
at Munir, might want to get involved in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:44.400
that the news flow around some of the vaccines&nbsp;
is not great across the water for a range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:49.360
reasons. And that’s both across in Europe and&nbsp;
in America. I’m just wondering what we can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.360 --> 00:25:53.600
to just again reassure people again, the&nbsp;
further down the age cohort you’re going,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:58.240
the more this becomes a factor. Reassure people&nbsp;
that you know, the vaccines are safe, and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:03.040
worth taking, and they do work. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Emily, could you reply to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:04.960
that now, do you think?
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:10.640
of course. So, and I think on the innovation and&nbsp;
creativity point, the point is well taken and one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:14.800
of the reasons for giving the local funding was&nbsp;
to encourage that innovation at a local level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:19.680
where people have a really good understanding of&nbsp;
what’s missing and what can be done to improve it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:27.920
We are doing the work both for the move into&nbsp;
cohorts, 10, 11 and 12, when we receive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:31.680
which will, you know, we’ll receive I hope&nbsp;
guidance from JCVI over the next couple of weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:37.440
about how to think about that prioritisation.
And we have, you know, campaigns planned and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:42.400
that kind of thing to increase vaccine uptake&nbsp;
among younger groups, which obviously, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:46.720
we’re not pushing out at the moment, because&nbsp;
April is going to be second dose month, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.720 --> 00:26:50.800
that push into younger cohorts will happen&nbsp;
later in the summer, or later in the spring.&nbsp;

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:57.520
But I take, completely take your point, we need&nbsp;
to continue to use the insights that we get every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.520 --> 00:27:02.640
day from the data to refocus our efforts and to&nbsp;
make sure we’re thinking about meeting people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:06.160
where they are, right. So rather than this&nbsp;
being convenient for us, what’s convenient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.160 --> 00:27:10.800
for the people who are receiving the vaccine,&nbsp;
what’s inspiring for them. And what I’ve seen as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:15.920
I’ve been out to visit sites and local systems is&nbsp;
tonnes of innovation. So we need to support people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:21.440
in doing that and then obviously provide the&nbsp;
national infrastructure to scale that, steal it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:25.600
you know, publicise it, make sure everybody knows&nbsp;
they’ve got permission to, you know, to extend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:29.520
these offers in innovative ways, so long as that’s&nbsp;
safe and within the existing clinical guidance.&nbsp;

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:36.400
On the news flow, shall I hand that to&nbsp;
one of our clinical colleagues, David?&nbsp;

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:40.960
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, Munir or Steve,&nbsp;
would you like to comment? Or Ara?&nbsp;

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:50.000
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed: I can comment. So it&nbsp;
does have, the risk benefit is critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:54.480
with any intervention such as a vaccine,&nbsp;
and this is being intensively monitored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:55.040 --> 00:28:01.440
throughout the world. The MHRA has been looking at&nbsp;
this every day in terms of reports coming in plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:06.160
also the effectiveness, working with partners&nbsp;
such as Public Health England and the NHS to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:13.280
make sure that the benefit risk is positive.
The EMA came out last week and said that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:20.960
benefit risk is very positive, and the vaccine is&nbsp;
safe and effective, and the FDA has done the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:27.120
as well. And, you know, overall, there is a huge&nbsp;
amount of work going on to continuously monitor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:33.840
the effectiveness and safety of the vaccine.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: I can concur with that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:41.040
Obviously, as with any vaccine or drug, the&nbsp;
two key things to look at are effectiveness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:49.440
and of course, safety is always paramount.&nbsp;
Vaccines in general are highly effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.400 --> 00:28:56.240
interventions, health interventions, and are also&nbsp;
extremely safe. We know that from many years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.240 --> 00:29:01.040
experience, and there’s no doubt these vaccines&nbsp;
are effective. That’s not only been shown in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:06.320
clinical trials that the various vaccines have&nbsp;
gone through and are continuing to go through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:11.840
but also in the real world experience here in&nbsp;
the UK, we are seeing data from Public Health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:18.080
England but other academic bodies too show&nbsp;
not just the reduction in infection rates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:24.160
but very importantly, a reduction in hospital&nbsp;
admissions. And I’m sure that will be translated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.160 --> 00:29:31.040
into deaths too. And of course, in other&nbsp;
countries, such as Israel, seeing the same.&nbsp;

00:29:31.760 --> 00:29:39.840
So it’s important to remember that COVID is a&nbsp;
serious virus that causes harm and death. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:44.480
of course, it’s not just death that we have to&nbsp;
worry about. It’s conditions such as long COVID,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:50.400
and although we will need to look at this as the&nbsp;
data emerges, there’s no reason to think that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:57.760
vaccines won’t be effective in preventing disease&nbsp;
and therefore, or preventing the virus infection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:02.560
and therefore preventing long COVID. So this is&nbsp;
about the full range of consequences at COVID.&nbsp;

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:06.560
And then, of course, as Munir said,&nbsp;
the clinical trials are designed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:11.440
test safety. That’s one of the key purposes of&nbsp;
clinical trials. But it doesn’t stop with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:17.360
clinical trials. Regulators such as the&nbsp;
MHRA, the EMA and the FDA are constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:24.480
looking at the safety of vaccines as they do&nbsp;
with all drugs after they have been licenced.&nbsp;

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:29.680
We have very, very well established systems in&nbsp;
the UK, the yellow card system is the one that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:41.040
best known, to ensure that the doctors but also&nbsp;
citizens can report in anything that they might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:47.440
consider a side effect of a vaccine or a drug and&nbsp;
the MHRA are looking at those all the time. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.080 --> 00:30:53.360
that is a constant process, and our&nbsp;
regulators here and in other countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.360 --> 00:30:57.280
are extremely experienced at doing&nbsp;
this, and very effective at doing it.&nbsp;

00:30:57.280 --> 00:31:00.960
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Steve. Ara, did you&nbsp;
want to add to that, or does that cover it?&nbsp;

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:05.760
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi of Denham: I think it’s you know,&nbsp;
it’s very adequately covered. And I must say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:10.960
I mean the FDA results recently, the American&nbsp;
data is even better than we’ve ever seen before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.960 --> 00:31:18.960
when it comes to the AZ vaccine, so, no, it’s a,&nbsp;
you know. I have had COVID. I don’t recommend it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:23.200
to anyone. So get your vaccine.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.200 --> 00:31:28.320
Ara. So Ian and Andrew. Ian Dodge.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you. I just wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:28.320 --> 00:31:34.160
to pick up on in the inequalities point. So&nbsp;
notwithstanding the challenge around differential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:41.440
uptake that still exists, certainly in the 28&nbsp;
years I’ve been working in health I’ve not seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.440 --> 00:31:49.920
a similar programme really forensically focus on&nbsp;
inequalities at the scale, with the level of data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.920 --> 00:31:54.240
and the level of innovation that we’ve&nbsp;
had in the COVID vaccination programme.&nbsp;

00:31:54.880 --> 00:31:58.960
And certainly, the national inequalities&nbsp;
team will think it sets a new benchmark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:04.480
actually, for what we should now be doing&nbsp;
across all of our programmes, wherever that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:12.720
cancer, early stage diagnosis, detecting&nbsp;
hypertension in the community, or looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:19.040
at differential waiting times for elective care.&nbsp;
And that’s exactly what the planning guidance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.040 --> 00:32:23.920
I think, provides a focus on how do we really&nbsp;
leverage out the great work you’ve been doing?&nbsp;

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:31.600
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ian. Andrew. Andrew Morris.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: Thanks, David. Emily, just to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:37.520
as we kind of look towards the autumn, do we&nbsp;
know yet whether we can give the booster jab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.240 --> 00:32:43.200
the booster COVID job along with the flu jab&nbsp;
at the same time, because clearly that would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:44.720
fantastic news if we can?&nbsp;

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:53.120
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: So there are clinical trials going&nbsp;
on at the moment that look at the coterminosity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:59.680
option, Andrew, but also the nature of what a&nbsp;
booster jab might look like and whether that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:07.360
a different vaccine, or a different vaccine type&nbsp;
and all kinds of variations of that. So those are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.360 --> 00:33:12.160
those are already in progress, and we’d expect&nbsp;
them to report over the sort of early to late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:12.160 --> 00:33:17.360
summer. I don’t know if Steve wants to come in&nbsp;
about, I’m not currently sure we’re going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:23.120
the data very much in advance of being able to&nbsp;
plan the autumn. So we have a base case scenario,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:29.520
and a variable scenario about whether we&nbsp;
can give flu at the same time or not. Steve?&nbsp;

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:35.680
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yes. So currently, the green&nbsp;
book, which is the handbook of vaccination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:40.080
produced by public health colleagues,&nbsp;
recommends a seven day interval between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:45.600
COVID vaccine and the flu vaccine. Clearly we’re&nbsp;
out of flu vaccination season at the moment. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.160 --> 00:33:51.920
that was a recommendation. That is not necessarily&nbsp;
based on studies because those studies haven’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:58.560
been done yet. It’s based on caution. But it is&nbsp;
quite required that those clinical studies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.560 --> 00:34:05.920
done. And there is no overriding reason to think&nbsp;
that it won’t be possible to co-administer, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:05.920 --> 00:34:12.080
we need to test that in a clinical setting. That’s&nbsp;
being done. Hopefully, we will get readouts in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:17.360
summer in time for the programme in the winter.
But I think there are other factors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:18.080 --> 00:34:26.160
in play around how the flu and COVID programmes&nbsp;
interact next winter, including whether, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.160 --> 00:34:33.520
long immunity lasts from, from current vaccines,&nbsp;
whether there will need to be new vaccines against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.520 --> 00:34:39.200
the various variants that are emerging. And all&nbsp;
that will play into how those programmes interact.&nbsp;

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:44.640
We are planning on the basis there will be an&nbsp;
interaction, but the scientific questions, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:49.600
are some scientific and clinical questions that&nbsp;
are outstanding that I don’t think we will know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:49.600 --> 00:34:56.000
about until we get nearer summer to really nail&nbsp;
down how the two programmes will work together.&nbsp;

00:34:56.000 --> 00:35:02.240
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Steve. So Emily, maybe&nbsp;
just one last question for me that if you had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:09.360
to pick out one thing which has really inspired&nbsp;
the success of this rollout programme, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.360 --> 00:35:14.000
what would it be?
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:21.760
That’s a really difficult question. When I give,&nbsp;
I’ve been talking to various other countries about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.760 --> 00:35:27.760
their, you know, sharing between our programme&nbsp;
and their programme, and I’ve actually, I actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.760 --> 00:35:31.760
found it on four things, David, if that’s okay,&nbsp;
but I’m happy to pick one of them out at the end.&nbsp;

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:35.760
The first is that we started from a great&nbsp;
foundation, both of the work the vaccines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:35.760 --> 00:35:40.080
taskforce did to bring the vaccines and create&nbsp;
this portfolio of vaccines into the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:47.360
and the work of HCVI and the MHRA as independent&nbsp;
bodies to really dynamically review the data and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:51.360
give that independent clinical advice. So we&nbsp;
started off with that very clear position.&nbsp;

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:56.480
The second was designing the programme from the&nbsp;
beginning along guidance from the World Health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:56.480 --> 00:36:00.800
Organization that said what does it take to make&nbsp;
large scale vaccination programmes successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.800 --> 00:36:05.360
which centred on having that really&nbsp;
simple set of guiding principles so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.360 --> 00:36:09.040
that the programme is perceived as fair and&nbsp;
straightforward, and obviously we got that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:09.040 --> 00:36:15.920
based on the guidance from MHRA and JCVI.
And then executing operationally brilliantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.920 --> 00:36:20.080
and as much as possible flawlessly, although&nbsp;
it’s not actually possible to do it flawlessly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:25.600
Because just in that act of operation&nbsp;
and the smooth experience people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:28.880
almost always have when they arrive&nbsp;
at a vaccine centre, the friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:28.880 --> 00:36:32.400
response they get when they arrive at a vaccine&nbsp;
centre, the good experience of booking at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:36.800
national booking service which Andrew outlined&nbsp;
in a previous board meeting, that all gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:36.800 --> 00:36:40.800
confidence both in the programme but obviously&nbsp;
then also in the vaccine by association.&nbsp;

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:46.800
So designing ourselves so that we could&nbsp;
deliver operationally effectively every day and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:51.280
learn every day from the data and then&nbsp;
improve for the following day. So we built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:56.000
that foundation of operational delivery based&nbsp;
on those World Health Organization principles.&nbsp;

00:36:57.680 --> 00:37:02.240
The third thing, which we’ve talked about in this&nbsp;
meeting is data, data, data. But not just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.240 --> 00:37:06.560
data, but the insight from the data. So designing&nbsp;
from the beginning a system which allowed us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:11.840
look every day at what was being delivered, to&nbsp;
take the learnings from that and then develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.400 --> 00:37:15.200
accordingly or amend our&nbsp;
approaches, as Ian outlined.&nbsp;

00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:20.240
And the final thing, which is the single thing&nbsp;
that I would pick out in answer to your question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:32.960
is the extraordinary mobilising force and&nbsp;
delivery force that the NHS is. The combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:39.760
the, the sort of the idea of the NHS, the&nbsp;
brand of the NHS, the people of the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:46.800
and that just unbelievable power of everybody&nbsp;
coming together, not just NHS people, although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:46.800 --> 00:37:52.320
it’s a lot of NHS people, but everybody in support&nbsp;
of the NHS, sort of whole communities, grouping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:57.120
around a local delivery centre to marshal cars in&nbsp;
the carpark and to book appointments and to check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:57.120 --> 00:38:02.320
people in and then to observe people while they’re&nbsp;
waiting, combined with the clinical leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:02.960 --> 00:38:07.120
delivering the vaccine itself or the pharmacists&nbsp;
making sure that it’s dispensed appropriately.&nbsp;

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:13.680
The whole kind of right from that micro&nbsp;
level to the national scale that we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:21.200
seen and that Simon outlined, I think&nbsp;
we’ve relied on all of the aspects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:21.200 --> 00:38:24.720
the NHS and the tens of thousands of people&nbsp;
that are delivering the programme every day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:30.160
That’s a major inspiring force every&nbsp;
day and something without which this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:34.720
delivery would have been completely impossible.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, Emily, that’s a wonderful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:42.160
answer. And I might add that this wouldn’t have&nbsp;
been done without your leadership of the programme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:48.240
as well. So again, we say this every time I know,&nbsp;
but thank you very much for everything you’ve done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.880 --> 00:38:55.360
to make this programme a success. Thank you.
So I think we’ll move on to the next item,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:55.360 --> 00:39:01.680
which is the COVID-19 response overview.&nbsp;
Simon, are you going to kick me off on off?&nbsp;

00:39:01.680 --> 00:39:04.560
&gt;&gt;Simon Eccles: Actually, I&nbsp;
think Steve is going to, David.&nbsp;

00:39:04.560 --> 00:39:07.680
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Steve, over to you.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yes, thank you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:14.080
David. So board members will be well aware that&nbsp;
two days ago was a very poignant day. It marked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.080 --> 00:39:21.040
the anniversary of the commencement of the first&nbsp;
lockdown in the UK as a response to the pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.000 --> 00:39:29.120
And I’m sure like everybody here today, those&nbsp;
of us that were in Skipton House spent a minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:29.920 --> 00:39:38.720
in the atrium, socially distanced, but to have a&nbsp;
minute’s silence and to reflect on the past year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.720 --> 00:39:44.640
and I know many around the country, particularly&nbsp;
in the NHS and our partners did that on Tuesday.&nbsp;

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:53.520
This paper sets out to provide an overview of the&nbsp;
challenges that the NHS has faced over that year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.720 --> 00:40:00.880
It sets out how we have planned and&nbsp;
responded to those challenges. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.880 --> 00:40:07.920
it is by its nature a high-level overview. It&nbsp;
can’t and doesn’t capture everything that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.920 --> 00:40:13.360
happened throughout the NHS, at a very local&nbsp;
level, at regional level at national level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:14.160 --> 00:40:21.040
But I know that what it does give a sense of&nbsp;
is just the tremendous effort that everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.040 --> 00:40:26.080
has put in over the last year and personally,&nbsp;
I have just been overwhelmed by the passion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.080 --> 00:40:32.480
the focus, the dedication, and actually the sheer&nbsp;
determination of colleagues throughout the NHS as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:37.280
they have responded to the pandemic&nbsp;
over the last year working together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:37.280 --> 00:40:41.920
to ensure that patients with COVID, and&nbsp;
of course patients with other conditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.560 --> 00:40:47.920
have continued to receive the care that they need.
I’m not going to go through the paper in detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:47.920 --> 00:40:52.320
because it’s there to be read but I&nbsp;
might just point out a few highlights.&nbsp;

00:40:53.440 --> 00:40:59.360
So the first is that although we’ve been&nbsp;
thinking about a year since March 23 last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:00.160 --> 00:41:05.760
preparations in the NHS for the pandemic started&nbsp;
a couple of months before, and as you will see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.760 --> 00:41:12.320
the paper and as you will recall the NHS stood&nbsp;
itself up to a level four incident response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:18.640
its highest level of incident at the end of&nbsp;
January, January 30 last year, and indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.640 --> 00:41:24.960
since that time, apart from a couple of months&nbsp;
in the late summer and early autumn, until today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:30.480
again, as you heard, the NHS has continued&nbsp;
to be in that level of incidence. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:30.480 --> 00:41:36.640
that’s unparalleled and it just shows the level of&nbsp;
intensity that the service has been working under.&nbsp;

00:41:37.280 --> 00:41:45.920
But it’s easy to forget that the prior to March&nbsp;
last year, the NHS was deeply involved in working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.920 --> 00:41:50.960
to support individuals through the pandemic.&nbsp;
And I would highlight here the work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:50.960 --> 00:41:57.920
the very early work of colleagues in Arrowe Park&nbsp;
on the Wirral, to ensure that we could quarantine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:59.120 --> 00:42:07.760
in a welcoming environment, those UK citizens&nbsp;
that were being repatriated from Wuhan and China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:07.760 --> 00:42:15.920
which was the epicentre of the initial pandemic.
The second point I’d make is, and again, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:15.920 --> 00:42:21.440
get the sense of this in the paper, that we have&nbsp;
taken the learning from the first wave in April,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.440 --> 00:42:26.720
and then translated that through into our&nbsp;
response to the second wave that we have seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:33.600
in November through to January. Different waves&nbsp;
in different parts of the country, January&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:33.600 --> 00:42:37.760
to February, time from the start, we’re beginning&nbsp;
to see numbers decrease substantially, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.760 --> 00:42:43.840
we’re still at the tail end of that second wave.
A few things I would highlight there. So firstly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.720 --> 00:42:49.600
when we had the first wave and preparation for&nbsp;
the first wave, in April, we stood down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:49.600 --> 00:42:55.200
substantial majority of elective care. And we did&nbsp;
that because at that time it was really unclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:55.920 --> 00:43:00.080
just how many patients we would have to deal&nbsp;
with. It was not certain how the public would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.080 --> 00:43:05.120
react to those social distancing measures&nbsp;
that the government had asked us all to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:11.440
And therefore, it was important that we created&nbsp;
the capacity to manage a large number of COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.440 --> 00:43:15.760
patients. And of course, we did that.
But as we moved to the second wave in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:15.760 --> 00:43:22.000
late autumn and through the winter, we could take&nbsp;
the lessons from that first wave. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:29.200
our data and understanding of this virus and&nbsp;
how infection rates were evolving locally was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:29.200 --> 00:43:33.760
much better. And therefore in the second wave,&nbsp;
we were able to keep elective services going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:41.200
in many parts of the country much more&nbsp;
effectively than unfortunately we had to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:45.040
in the first wave. And I think that, again, is&nbsp;
testament to the planning over the summer, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:49.600
to the hard work of NHS staff.
And then the second point I’d make is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.600 --> 00:43:56.080
that we learned a lot about how to treat patients&nbsp;
with COVID, particularly those who require ITU, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:56.080 --> 00:44:00.640
expanded ITU facilities over the summer.&nbsp;
But we also learned about how you provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:00.640 --> 00:44:08.640
oxygen treatments to patients, such that we&nbsp;
didn’t necessarily have to ventilate patients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:08.640 --> 00:44:13.120
that we needed to ventilate in the first wave.
So that clinical and operational learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:17.040
went into second wave and along with&nbsp;
something else I’ll mention in a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:22.640
you can quite clearly see that the proportion&nbsp;
of patients who needed intensive care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.640 --> 00:44:27.280
compared to a general bed in the second&nbsp;
wave was very different from the first wave.&nbsp;

00:44:28.480 --> 00:44:34.960
The third thing I’d say is that although this&nbsp;
paper focuses on the NHS in England, this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:34.960 --> 00:44:40.160
worldwide pandemic. Of course, pandemics are by&nbsp;
their nature global, and I think we should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:46.080
proud of some of the things that we’ve done in the&nbsp;
NHS which will benefit patients everywhere. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:51.120
the benefits of the National Health Service is&nbsp;
that you can stand up clinical trials rapidly at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:56.480
pace and at scale. And indeed, that has been done&nbsp;
to the recovery trial and its various components.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:57.280 --> 00:45:04.080
And fairly early on, very early on actually, in&nbsp;
the course of the pandemic that trial identified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:04.080 --> 00:45:12.480
dexamethasone, an existing drug and a cheap drug,&nbsp;
as an effective treatment for people with COVID.&nbsp;

00:45:12.480 --> 00:45:18.800
And by our calculations that’s already saved&nbsp;
around 22,000 lives in the UK, and we believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:18.800 --> 00:45:25.600
probably around a million throughout the world.
And I think that is a really clear example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.600 --> 00:45:34.560
how we can organise flexibly at pace within the&nbsp;
health service and identify benefits which can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:34.560 --> 00:45:40.240
then rapidly be expanded around the world.
But of course, I won’t go into any detail, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.240 --> 00:45:47.360
second example it is a vaccine programme, which&nbsp;
we just been talking about. And we deployed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:48.400 --> 00:45:54.240
vaccines here particularly effectively and I know&nbsp;
other parts of the world will be looking at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:54.240 --> 00:46:00.240
methodology that we’ve used in that and looking at&nbsp;
how they can translate it into their own settings.&nbsp;

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:06.480
The fourth point I’d make is that&nbsp;
although this paper isn’t focused,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:06.480 --> 00:46:13.200
well, it’s focused on the response to COVID,&nbsp;
as I’ve already hinted, we have during the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:19.760
kept services going for all patients. And&nbsp;
that hasn’t just meant that we’ve provided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:19.760 --> 00:46:26.160
services as usual, but we’ve continued to work&nbsp;
on long term plan commitments as best we can.&nbsp;

00:46:27.040 --> 00:46:33.520
And so it hasn’t been a year of focusing on&nbsp;
COVID to the expense of everything else. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:33.520 --> 00:46:40.160
have certainly tried throughout the year to&nbsp;
ensure that we continue to work towards improving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:43.680
healthcare and the health of the population.
And if I could point out one area which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:43.680 --> 00:46:47.760
mentioned in the paper, which is an area&nbsp;
that I have responsibility for, it’s in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:52.160
creation of the integrated stroke&nbsp;
networks, the delivery networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:52.720 --> 00:46:58.400
that is a key component of the stroke programme&nbsp;
in the long term plan, and there are many other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:58.400 --> 00:47:04.720
examples of that. So finally, what next, this is&nbsp;
a paper that looks back, but right at the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:04.720 --> 00:47:11.360
there is a few paragraphs looking forward. And&nbsp;
again, I won’t go into this in huge detail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:11.360 --> 00:47:17.520
but I think it is important to acknowledge that&nbsp;
like many countries around the world, whether it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:17.520 --> 00:47:24.000
the United States through our European neighbours,&nbsp;
services have unfortunately been disrupted during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:30.880
the pandemic. And we’ve certainly seen that here.
And so going forward, we need to recover those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.880 --> 00:47:36.720
services. We did a great job as you, as again,&nbsp;
the paper documents between the first wave and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.720 --> 00:47:40.400
the second wave, but there is clearly more work&nbsp;
to be done. That will be covered elsewhere in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.400 --> 00:47:46.240
board meeting today. But it’s very important&nbsp;
that we do that. And I know our staff here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:50.160
it’s very important as well, because&nbsp;
fundamentally, we get up in the morning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:50.160 --> 00:47:52.960
to do our very best for patients.
But I think it’s also important to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:58.400
acknowledge that in doing that our staff have&nbsp;
gone through an incredibly pressured year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.960 --> 00:48:06.320
They are in many cases exhausted, tired, and&nbsp;
they need to recover too. And people recovery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:06.320 --> 00:48:11.600
our staff recovery needs to be at heart of service&nbsp;
recovery, because it’s when our staff are working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.600 --> 00:48:16.240
at their best and feeling recovered that they&nbsp;
will be able to do their best for patients.&nbsp;

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:21.280
COVID is going to be here for us beyond this last&nbsp;
year. It’s really something that we will need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:21.280 --> 00:48:26.640
learn to live with. There will be many ups and&nbsp;
downs, I’m sure during this year and beyond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:27.360 --> 00:48:33.840
But, David, I think this paper gives a good&nbsp;
overview of our response so far. Thank you.&nbsp;

00:48:33.840 --> 00:48:37.040
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Thank you very&nbsp;
much, Steve, it is a terrific paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:37.920 --> 00:48:44.000
Could I ask Ruth, Ruth, if you’d like to come in?
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thanks very much. Thanks, chairman. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:50.720
agree completely with what Steve has just outlined&nbsp;
in a lot of detail, actually. So thank you for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:50.720 --> 00:48:55.840
that. The only bits of addition I would say is&nbsp;
that I’ve been really impressed on this last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:55.840 --> 00:49:03.440
couple of months, over a year rather about the&nbsp;
team work that people have approached this. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:03.440 --> 00:49:09.520
been not just individuals, it’s been how teams&nbsp;
have come together. And actually, hierarchies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:09.520 --> 00:49:14.560
have gone in many ways. And people from across all&nbsp;
sorts of different disciplines have come around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:14.560 --> 00:49:18.880
come together around the problem and worked&nbsp;
together. And that includes our students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:19.600 --> 00:49:23.440
medical students, student nurses,&nbsp;
student midwives have stepped up too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:23.440 --> 00:49:28.560
as well as our volunteers. And of course, the&nbsp;
people who have come out of retirement, and I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:28.560 --> 00:49:34.560
been very grateful to all of those and worked&nbsp;
alongside many of them during this last year.&nbsp;

00:49:34.560 --> 00:49:43.520
So my personal thanks to all of our NHS and&nbsp;
care staff that have worked together to support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:43.520 --> 00:49:51.200
the NHS and care sector during this last year.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ruth and Steve, Ara, you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:51.200 --> 00:49:55.680
putting your hand up. Sorry, I couldn’t see you.
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi of Denham: Yeah, I must say, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:55.680 --> 00:50:01.360
as a board member just to acknowledge&nbsp;
what Steve said. I mean, if you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:01.360 --> 00:50:08.240
the clinical trial contribution, 1 million&nbsp;
NHS patients were recruited into a trial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:08.240 --> 00:50:14.960
That’s an unbelievable achievement. Even the New&nbsp;
York Times wrote about this. You know, it puts the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:14.960 --> 00:50:21.360
whole of the, you know, the Western Hemisphere,&nbsp;
the UK’s contribution to at least two or three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:21.360 --> 00:50:28.800
major discoveries of therapeutics that have&nbsp;
had a huge impact on mortality post-COVID.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:29.600 --> 00:50:35.600
Steve mentioned one of them. And that’s something&nbsp;
that this country not should be proud of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:35.600 --> 00:50:42.320
but also the NHS should be proud of, but how could&nbsp;
we keep this? How can we keep this post COVID?&nbsp;

00:50:42.320 --> 00:50:51.920
You know, this is the system that could do large&nbsp;
trials of that nature. I think also, just, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:51.920 --> 00:50:56.560
people forget, by the way, even us who&nbsp;
work in the NHS sometimes don’t get this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.560 --> 00:51:01.840
you know, Steve has said in the paper, twice&nbsp;
as many non-COVID patients were treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:03.120 --> 00:51:08.960
during all the peaks throughout the whole period&nbsp;
since COVID started. There is this perception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:08.960 --> 00:51:14.960
that everything was COVID. But it wasn’t. And&nbsp;
that’s again, big major congratulations to those.&nbsp;

00:51:15.920 --> 00:51:19.680
On the trial side, by the way, it&nbsp;
wasn’t the scientists who did trials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:19.680 --> 00:51:25.120
It was NHS doctors and nurses, and management&nbsp;
who supported these, and this is the bit that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:25.120 --> 00:51:30.800
we forget. It’s the doctor who sees the patients&nbsp;
who decides who needs to be recruited into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:30.800 --> 00:51:35.440
trial, and we need to acknowledge that. Not the&nbsp;
trial, what we call the principal investigator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:35.440 --> 00:51:39.600
which obviously they have an important role.&nbsp;
But that’s, that’s tremendous. And I hope that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:39.600 --> 00:51:44.960
we will keep building the public confidence&nbsp;
of what the NHS could do in terms of trials.&nbsp;

00:51:44.960 --> 00:51:48.400
Also, by the way, some of the&nbsp;
stuff that NHS England commissioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:51.280 --> 00:52:00.320
through like, you know, COVID, the virtual&nbsp;
COVID wards in the community, the COVID pathway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:05.760
the pulse oximeter, that is innovation, that is&nbsp;
really, truly innovation, which has had an impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:05.760 --> 00:52:12.080
in the, more impact in the second peak. So these&nbsp;
are things that we should celebrate and remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:13.920 --> 00:52:22.000
because at a time of serious strain, that is where&nbsp;
the creativity of the NHS staff really did shine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:23.520 --> 00:52:27.840
across the system in the UK and globally.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, thank you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:27.840 --> 00:52:35.200
Ara. I think it’s really shown the benefits of&nbsp;
a big single payer integrated system. And also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:35.200 --> 00:52:42.000
the fact that the extraordinary cooperation and&nbsp;
partnership between the pharmaceutical industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:47.040
academia, the government, and the&nbsp;
healthcare system, which is I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:47.040 --> 00:52:51.600
pretty much unparalleled, and certainly in&nbsp;
my time in the NHS, which is now around 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:51.600 --> 00:52:56.960
years. I’ve got Ian Dodge and Munir.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:56.960 --> 00:53:02.560
So, further elaboration on the teamwork&nbsp;
point. Certainly the NHS England board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:02.560 --> 00:53:09.120
came together in January 2019 to discuss&nbsp;
our five-year plans for general practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.680 --> 00:53:16.800
including then the very first national&nbsp;
funding for primary care networks. And we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:16.800 --> 00:53:22.240
big ambitions for how they would evolve over a&nbsp;
five-year period. And I think one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:22.240 --> 00:53:29.040
that the pandemic has massively demonstrated&nbsp;
is the critical importance of the PCN platform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:35.280
not least in relation to the last agenda&nbsp;
item around vaccine delivery. And rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:36.480 --> 00:53:42.160
the pandemic response meaning that we have&nbsp;
to defer a number of our key long term plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:42.160 --> 00:53:48.640
deliverables, that is a really, I think, brilliant&nbsp;
example of how it has hugely helped accelerate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:49.200 --> 00:53:54.080
practical development. Thank you, chair.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ian. And Munir.&nbsp;

00:53:54.080 --> 00:53:59.840
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed: Thanks very much, chair. So&nbsp;
I completely agree with what Ara has just said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:59.840 --> 00:54:05.200
but I just wanted to thank members of the public&nbsp;
and patients admitted to hospital taking part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:05.200 --> 00:54:09.280
in the trials. Without them, we wouldn’t&nbsp;
have achieved any of this. So we really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:09.280 --> 00:54:14.400
do we need to thank the members of the public.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: That’s an excellent point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:14.400 --> 00:54:20.320
Munir. There was a, as Ara said, there’s over a&nbsp;
million people who have been involved in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:20.320 --> 00:54:25.920
trials and that’s extraordinary. David Roberts.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So I think there will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:25.920 --> 00:54:31.120
tonnes of lessons for us to learn and lots of&nbsp;
ways in which we can accelerate change. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:31.760 --> 00:54:36.960
I think we need to really think hard about we take&nbsp;
those lessons and bring them into our everyday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:36.960 --> 00:54:41.760
operating practices, whether that’s how we&nbsp;
make decisions, whether it’s the power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:45.600
system working, whether it’s the use&nbsp;
of technology, etc, etc. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:52.880
we really need to be quite intentional about&nbsp;
how we bring those learnings into our sort of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:52.880 --> 00:54:57.200
because to some extent, the biggest challenge&nbsp;
is perhaps ahead of us, which is recovery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:57.200 --> 00:55:02.240
And we’ll come on to that later.
There’s just a brief, very brief angle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:02.240 --> 00:55:07.280
David, and it’s a story. And it comes back to&nbsp;
teamworking, but it’s also about culture. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:07.280 --> 00:55:12.640
watching a television programme, I think it was&nbsp;
about a hospital in East London, but I’m not sure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:12.640 --> 00:55:18.080
I think it was Clive Myrie who did a report.&nbsp;
And there was a gentleman there who in in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:18.080 --> 00:55:24.320
normal circumstances would be called a cleaner.&nbsp;
And he was asked, what do you do? And he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:24.320 --> 00:55:30.960
I’m part of the infection control team. My job is&nbsp;
to make sure that this place is free of infection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:31.520 --> 00:55:36.640
to allow the doctors and nurses to look&nbsp;
after people and make them make them better.&nbsp;

00:55:36.640 --> 00:55:42.880
And you know what, really struck a chord with&nbsp;
me. I’d like to say thank you to that gentleman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:42.880 --> 00:55:47.600
and to all the other gentlemen and ladies&nbsp;
in that sort of part of the porters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.600 --> 00:55:55.440
the cleaners, they’re all part of the infection&nbsp;
control and the patient sort of service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:56.080 --> 00:56:01.200
and they’re all quite often forgotten. And yet&nbsp;
they are critical. Things don’t work without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:01.200 --> 00:56:05.760
them. It’s true in general practice as well. So&nbsp;
thank you to them, that part of the team as well.&nbsp;

00:56:06.480 --> 00:56:10.560
&gt;&gt;David Prior: That’s a very excellent point,&nbsp;
David, and Prerana, I know you’re on the call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:10.560 --> 00:56:16.480
as well. And I think, I mean, this is what&nbsp;
inclusion is all about, actually. So I’m sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:17.120 --> 00:56:21.040
you would agree with that. Are you there, Prerana?
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Yes, I am, David. Thank you. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:21.040 --> 00:56:25.280
thanks, David, for saying that, because one of the&nbsp;
things that we’d focused on and everything we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:25.280 --> 00:56:33.440
done is exactly that. Equity of access, equity of&nbsp;
involvement, of information. I had convened and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:33.440 --> 00:56:38.560
have regular discussions with an estates and&nbsp;
facilities roundtable for precisely the group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:38.560 --> 00:56:44.640
of people that you’ve just mentioned, David. And,&nbsp;
you know, this is whether it was staff protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:44.640 --> 00:56:51.200
which is about, you know, PPE, training,&nbsp;
information, and now, staff protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:51.200 --> 00:56:58.000
through the vaccine uptake, every member of the&nbsp;
NHS team is absolutely crucial and critical.&nbsp;

00:56:58.000 --> 00:57:03.440
And one of the enduring legacies of our COVID&nbsp;
response across the NHS is going to be actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:03.440 --> 00:57:11.920
how it is one team, and all of the 350 roles&nbsp;
across the NHS make it all work and create the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:11.920 --> 00:57:18.000
patient outcomes that the team has created over&nbsp;
this year. So I would like to add my thanks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:18.000 --> 00:57:22.720
just reiterate that the person who you met from&nbsp;
the infection prevention and control team, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:22.720 --> 00:57:28.400
know, I love that as well. It’s our equivalent of,&nbsp;
you know, the janitor at NASA putting the man on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:28.400 --> 00:57:32.960
the moon, and that’s really inspiring. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, thanks Prerana. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:32.960 --> 00:57:39.840
Steve, I don’t know if you want to just&nbsp;
have a last word on this before we move on?&nbsp;

00:57:39.840 --> 00:57:46.160
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: No, I think I think the team&nbsp;
point that Ruth made and others have built on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:46.160 --> 00:57:52.560
I think is incredibly important. And, you know,&nbsp;
we’ve always worked in teams, and those of us in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:52.560 --> 00:57:57.600
the health service know that that team is a very&nbsp;
extensive team. I think at one point, I think it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:57.600 --> 00:58:01.920
might have been at one of the press conferences, I&nbsp;
talked about when I was at the Royal Free managing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:02.640 --> 00:58:10.320
part of the response to Ebola in the Ebola unit&nbsp;
there, it was the engineers that kept the huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:10.320 --> 00:58:16.480
plant that is required to do the air cleaning&nbsp;
for those beds, who were some of the key people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:16.480 --> 00:58:20.320
the unsung heroes. And the Evening Standard,&nbsp;
I remember, took a photograph of the team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:21.040 --> 00:58:25.120
and there were about 40 people involved in it. And&nbsp;
of course, those that were left out, and it just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:25.120 --> 00:58:31.040
emphasises that this is not doctors and nurses,&nbsp;
allied health professionals, it’s managers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:31.040 --> 00:58:39.520
it’s porters, it’s the engineers, it’s catering&nbsp;
teams, it’s everybody pulling together and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:40.080 --> 00:58:44.160
we should be proud of all of them.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Steve, I know you won’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:44.160 --> 00:58:49.600
take this the wrong way when I say that I think we&nbsp;
all hope that the less we see you on television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:49.600 --> 00:58:54.320
the better going forward. And I mean that in&nbsp;
the nicest possible way, because it means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:54.960 --> 00:58:58.720
the pandemic will be over. But you’ve&nbsp;
done a wonderful job representing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:58.720 --> 00:59:04.000
the NHS and the country as a whole over&nbsp;
the last 12 months, much appreciated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:04.000 --> 00:59:08.080
So thank you very much.
So if we move on then to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:08.080 --> 00:59:14.720
the operational and financial update, Amanda.
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you, David. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:14.720 --> 00:59:20.160
think this picks up very well actually from the&nbsp;
conversation we’ve just had. Because as we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:20.160 --> 00:59:27.760
just noted, the NHS has been through actually&nbsp;
the most pressured period over this last quarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:27.760 --> 00:59:33.600
because of COVID than we’ve seen actually&nbsp;
over the whole of the year since COVID began.&nbsp;

00:59:34.480 --> 00:59:39.440
I know everyone’s very familiar with the&nbsp;
figures but 34,000 inpatients at peak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:39.440 --> 00:59:45.280
4,000 patients, COVID positive patients in&nbsp;
our critical care facilities. And as Ara said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:45.280 --> 00:59:49.360
and I think this was such an important point&nbsp;
and thank you for making it, still of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:49.360 --> 00:59:54.400
treating two non-COVID patients for every&nbsp;
one inpatient with COVID through that period.&nbsp;

00:59:56.720 --> 01:00:01.920
I think it’s worth just taking a moment to&nbsp;
build on what Steve and others have said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:01.920 --> 01:00:11.200
about what it was that enabled the NHS to do&nbsp;
what it has over the last quarter. And clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:11.760 --> 01:00:17.600
the absolute answer to that is the extraordinary&nbsp;
dedication of staff as we’ve just discussed from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:17.600 --> 01:00:21.520
all different backgrounds, and all parts&nbsp;
of the health service coming together.&nbsp;

01:00:22.320 --> 01:00:25.760
But probably worth just highlighting&nbsp;
a number of other things actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:25.760 --> 01:00:32.400
sort of working between the two papers.&nbsp;
So what we did centrally is invested&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:33.040 --> 01:00:40.960
237 million in critical care capacity. That’s&nbsp;
proved invaluable through the last few months&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:40.960 --> 01:00:47.680
as we’ve seen the pressures on critical care in&nbsp;
particular rise. A 10 million plus specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:47.680 --> 01:00:53.760
for support for critical care staff, some of the&nbsp;
colleagues who’ve been, you know, hardest hit by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:53.760 --> 01:00:58.320
some of the pressures over the last period.
In relation to urgent emergency care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:58.320 --> 01:01:05.120
as Pauline has said before, 450 million invested&nbsp;
in A&amp;E capital projects to improve capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:05.120 --> 01:01:11.600
and flow. We’ve had a significant,&nbsp;
led by Prerana and others, support for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:11.600 --> 01:01:19.760
our staff in place over this period, health and&nbsp;
wellbeing support accessed over 780,000 times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:19.760 --> 01:01:26.400
and the establishment of 40 mental health hubs.
We’ve also seen our workforce grow, of course, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:26.400 --> 01:01:33.920
been delighted to welcome 46,000 additional whole&nbsp;
time equivalent staff. And as Ruth said earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:33.920 --> 01:01:39.440
that doesn’t include, of course, the extraordinary&nbsp;
and invaluable contribution from our students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:40.080 --> 01:01:45.120
as well as those people who have returned to&nbsp;
work in the NHS having previously moved on or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:45.120 --> 01:01:52.240
retired. We’ve needed the capacity that we’ve been&nbsp;
able to secure in the independent sector. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:52.240 --> 01:01:57.840
it’s proved invaluable over the last few months,&nbsp;
and played a particularly important role actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:57.840 --> 01:02:01.840
in supporting some of our most urgent work. And&nbsp;
I want to say a word about that in a moment.&nbsp;

01:02:02.560 --> 01:02:07.600
And again, others have said it, so I won’t&nbsp;
repeat, but some of the work that colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:07.600 --> 01:02:12.880
in primary care, Ian, Matthew Wynne have&nbsp;
led the development of new services like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:12.880 --> 01:02:18.320
the oximetry at home, the COVID virtual wards,&nbsp;
estimated I think Ian that’s now benefited over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:18.320 --> 01:02:23.680
100,000 patients since those were set up.&nbsp;
And that doesn’t include of course again&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:23.680 --> 01:02:29.520
the support for discharge, which we know has made&nbsp;
a huge difference in keeping beds available for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:29.520 --> 01:02:34.480
those inpatients who needed them and supporting&nbsp;
people effectively at home equivalent. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:34.480 --> 01:02:39.520
we’re now saying of over 6,000 beds freed up as&nbsp;
a consequence of that funding and those services.&nbsp;

01:02:40.080 --> 01:02:45.600
So all of that package has, I think, been in place&nbsp;
to support our extraordinary staff to do what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:45.600 --> 01:02:51.040
have been able to do over the course of the last&nbsp;
few months. But I just wanted to pick out if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:51.040 --> 01:02:57.360
may, one particular thing that I think is also&nbsp;
worthy of note, which is the ability that the NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:57.360 --> 01:03:03.840
is shown to maintain its most urgent services over&nbsp;
that period for non-COVID care. And in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:03.840 --> 01:03:12.480
I’m thinking really of cancer. So by December, we&nbsp;
were seeing urgent cancer referrals and treatments&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:12.480 --> 01:03:19.920
at a level that was higher than pre-pandemic&nbsp;
levels. So that I think is testament to again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:19.920 --> 01:03:24.080
the great work that the national cancer team has&nbsp;
done supported by cancer alliances throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:24.080 --> 01:03:30.000
country and clinicians on the ground, as well&nbsp;
as our colleagues in primary care encouraging&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:35.120
people to come forward, referring them on. And&nbsp;
it gives us a very different platform as we think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:35.120 --> 01:03:40.880
about recovery than we would otherwise have had.
But now and again, Andrew, you called it out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:40.880 --> 01:03:46.400
earlier, now that the pressure from COVID is&nbsp;
reducing, thank goodness, though we’re conscious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:46.400 --> 01:03:51.920
of course, of the fact that it hasn’t gone&nbsp;
away and isn’t going to anytime soon, we do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:51.920 --> 01:03:59.680
turn our attention absolutely to the challenge&nbsp;
of recovery. That is particularly challenging in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:59.680 --> 01:04:11.360
relation to elective services. So we have figures&nbsp;
that are now I think worthy of pause when we think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:11.360 --> 01:04:19.200
that we’ve got 300,000 people who are now waiting&nbsp;
over a year. I mean, that is just in itself, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:19.200 --> 01:04:24.880
makes you realise the scale of the challenge ahead&nbsp;
of us. But I don’t want to just make the mistake&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:24.880 --> 01:04:32.160
of allowing us just to think about the elective&nbsp;
challenge, because we also have a recovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:32.160 --> 01:04:37.200
challenge in relation to mental health, learning&nbsp;
disabilities, and in relation to primary care&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:37.200 --> 01:04:43.120
and community services. No part of the NHS has&nbsp;
been untouched by the experience of the past year.&nbsp;

01:04:43.760 --> 01:04:48.480
But David, I wasn’t going to take us through the&nbsp;
detail of the paper because I think hopefully it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:48.480 --> 01:04:53.840
speaks for itself. But I did want to just&nbsp;
hand over to Pauline to say a few words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:53.840 --> 01:04:59.520
particularly about the approach to recovery.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Thank you, David. Amanda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:59.520 --> 01:05:04.640
if you don’t mind, I’ll just start off by saying&nbsp;
a little bit more about emergency care. Because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:04.640 --> 01:05:11.760
think I’m very mindful following the comments made&nbsp;
by Steve and indeed by Ara that throughout all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:11.760 --> 01:05:17.920
this period, emergency care and indeed elective&nbsp;
care and all of the staff who work in those areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:17.920 --> 01:05:23.280
have continued to really deliver what can&nbsp;
only be described as sort of operational&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:23.280 --> 01:05:30.320
excellence whilst focusing on transformation&nbsp;
on new ways of working, on new models of care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:30.320 --> 01:05:35.520
And I know that those staff would also want&nbsp;
us to acknowledge the other stakeholders that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:35.520 --> 01:05:42.080
helped them, the returners, the volunteers, etc.
But just one or two points around emergency care&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:42.080 --> 01:05:48.240
that clearly we are now seeing numbers&nbsp;
increasing. We have seen a significant drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:48.240 --> 01:05:54.240
in attendances over the period of the year. But&nbsp;
if we look at February’s numbers, we can see an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:54.240 --> 01:06:00.320
8% increase on January and likewise admissions&nbsp;
are starting to increase. But our four-hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:00.320 --> 01:06:07.360
performance still remains better than it was&nbsp;
this time last year and it’s been for some time.&nbsp;

01:06:07.360 --> 01:06:13.680
And also I think it’s worthwhile calling&nbsp;
out the excellent work done by 111 and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:13.680 --> 01:06:21.680
launch of 111 First. And if we look back over&nbsp;
the course of the year to January last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:21.680 --> 01:06:29.840
we’ve had an increase of about 3.2 million calls,&nbsp;
etc. We established the COVID response service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:29.840 --> 01:06:37.520
and really maintaining excellent call handling&nbsp;
times and increasing the use of online 111.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:38.400 --> 01:06:45.120
Now 34% of people who contact 111 who&nbsp;
are sent to an emergency department,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:46.080 --> 01:06:51.120
are actually given a booked appointment. And I&nbsp;
think that that is truly transformational. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:51.120 --> 01:06:59.280
we expect those numbers to increase as the service&nbsp;
is rolled out and as more resource goes into it.&nbsp;

01:06:59.840 --> 01:07:04.720
I suppose interesting to note, and maybe&nbsp;
just to call it out while we’re on the topic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:05.360 --> 01:07:11.200
the significant decrease in the number of&nbsp;
people contacting 111 over this period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:11.200 --> 01:07:15.600
with winter illness down about 84%.&nbsp;
I think it’s just worthy of note.&nbsp;

01:07:15.600 --> 01:07:18.160
But just one or two other&nbsp;
quick things on emergency care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:18.720 --> 01:07:24.720
calling out the performance of the ambulance&nbsp;
service, that year to date, all six standards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:25.440 --> 01:07:29.200
the delivery against them has been&nbsp;
higher than in the preceding year&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:29.200 --> 01:07:36.160
and real progress being made around see and treat,&nbsp;
hear and treat, non-conveyancing to hospital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:36.720 --> 01:07:43.840
I think the ambulance services, all 11 of them&nbsp;
are a real exemplar as far as mutual aid, working&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:43.840 --> 01:07:50.880
together, and indeed working with other emergency&nbsp;
services to support them during difficult times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:50.880 --> 01:07:57.120
Amanda’s already mentioned capital.
Just moving on to what I was asked to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:57.760 --> 01:08:05.040
around elective care. Clearly, as Amanda’s set&nbsp;
out, our waiting list is about 4.5 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:05.600 --> 01:08:14.000
And as Amanda mentioned, 52 weeks around 300,000&nbsp;
at this point. So clearly, that’s an area&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:14.000 --> 01:08:22.480
that is taking our focus on a day-to-day basis,&nbsp;
and I think throughout the whole of the NHS.&nbsp;

01:08:23.440 --> 01:08:29.440
As far as recovery is concerned, we will have&nbsp;
the benefit, we truly believe we will have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:29.440 --> 01:08:35.120
benefit of our new operational arrangements,&nbsp;
and that we will deliver elective recovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:35.120 --> 01:08:41.920
through those systems etc. So basically providers,&nbsp;
commissioners working together within a system&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:42.560 --> 01:08:48.960
in order to look at the waiting list, mutual&nbsp;
aid between them, and indeed systems working&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.960 --> 01:08:55.680
together within regions and further afield.
Our focus right now is on looking at the amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:55.680 --> 01:09:02.240
of work that our systems will be able to do&nbsp;
over the course of the next three to six months&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:02.240 --> 01:09:09.440
whilst absolutely acknowledging the fact that our&nbsp;
staff will need some downtime. And I’m sure like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:09.440 --> 01:09:15.680
all of us, they will be looking forward to some&nbsp;
sunny days when they can take a break from work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:15.680 --> 01:09:22.560
but looking at the work that can be done, and then&nbsp;
also having some additional investment available&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:22.560 --> 01:09:29.680
through the billion pounds to see the total&nbsp;
amount of work that can be done to ensure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:29.680 --> 01:09:36.640
we are addressing the needs of our most urgent&nbsp;
patients, our P1, P2 patients, but also thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:37.200 --> 01:09:42.560
carefully system by system as to the work&nbsp;
that can be done with regards to long waits.&nbsp;

01:09:43.680 --> 01:09:50.160
The independent sector will remain part of that&nbsp;
picture, and indeed working with a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:50.160 --> 01:09:57.840
stakeholders, including our colleges, etc,&nbsp;
on clinical validation, and on ensuring that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:57.840 --> 01:10:03.840
we have constant review of all patients.
We’re also working through our elective&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:03.840 --> 01:10:11.200
task force with patient organisations etc to&nbsp;
really understand how we can best communicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:12.160 --> 01:10:16.960
where we are with individual patients&nbsp;
as far as their care is concerned when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:16.960 --> 01:10:23.040
they are having to wait longer than they may&nbsp;
have had to do in the past. So I think we’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:23.040 --> 01:10:29.840
say a lot more about elective recovery in future&nbsp;
meetings. But that’s just quick summary for today.&nbsp;

01:10:30.720 --> 01:10:36.160
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Pauline.&nbsp;
Amanda, I wonder whether you could give us any&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:37.040 --> 01:10:41.440
information, a bit more information&nbsp;
on mental health. I mean, anecdotally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:41.440 --> 01:10:46.160
I think we know that the mental health of&nbsp;
the nation has deteriorated considerably&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:46.160 --> 01:10:51.200
over the last year. Have we got any, and I know we&nbsp;
are planning to spend more money on mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:51.200 --> 01:10:54.960
and provide more services, but&nbsp;
have we got any, is there any hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:55.520 --> 01:11:01.760
sort of assessment of the mental health issues&nbsp;
that we are now facing as we come out of, at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:01.760 --> 01:11:08.000
least slowly come out of the pandemic?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you, David. It may&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:08.000 --> 01:11:13.680
be that Julian wants to come in specifically on&nbsp;
the funding settlement for mental health. So it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:13.680 --> 01:11:19.200
500 million for both services and workforce. But&nbsp;
I think let’s let Julian speak more about that.&nbsp;

01:11:20.080 --> 01:11:24.960
In terms of the impact on&nbsp;
mental health, so we saw after&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:27.200 --> 01:11:34.080
first wave of COVID an immediate increase in&nbsp;
demand for mental health services. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:34.080 --> 01:11:39.520
we know from all of the evidence internationally&nbsp;
around trauma that you would expect if you like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:39.520 --> 01:11:46.640
a sort of second surge about 18 months later.&nbsp;
So, of course, as you expect, you’re beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:46.640 --> 01:11:52.160
to see that again. So the immediate demands&nbsp;
on mental health services haven’t gone away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.160 --> 01:11:55.760
they’ve continued, they’re growing. But&nbsp;
I think the thing that our mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:55.760 --> 01:12:01.680
colleagues would want us to be aware of is that&nbsp;
we need to be thinking about this is a long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:01.680 --> 01:12:06.720
not just a short term increase in demand. And the&nbsp;
other thing that I think, again, our mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:06.720 --> 01:12:11.600
colleagues would want us to be really thoughtful&nbsp;
about is the impact on children and young people.&nbsp;

01:12:12.160 --> 01:12:16.640
So that is the area where I think&nbsp;
now that schools have returned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:16.640 --> 01:12:21.200
we would be looking to see an impact. I think&nbsp;
there are early signs, we are beginning to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:21.200 --> 01:12:28.560
again, increase in demand. And that is at the&nbsp;
most acute end that’s on the tier four children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:29.840 --> 01:12:36.640
the inpatient capacity, which is an area that the&nbsp;
team are giving a lot of focus to at the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:36.640 --> 01:12:42.400
because that is under significant pressure. Eating&nbsp;
disorders is another area that we’re really seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:42.400 --> 01:12:48.000
increasing demand around, and that’s on top of the&nbsp;
kind of community, just if you like the slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:48.000 --> 01:12:54.080
more routine community presentation.
So there are figures in here about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:54.080 --> 01:12:58.640
impact on things like the waiting times&nbsp;
for IAPT, but the critical thing for us&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:58.640 --> 01:13:02.960
is I think maintaining the commitment to&nbsp;
the mental health investment standard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:02.960 --> 01:13:08.720
making sure the money gets where it needs to. And&nbsp;
then it’s the question about how we use the extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:08.720 --> 01:13:14.400
monies that government have allocated, and how&nbsp;
we prepare ourselves, as I say, for that future&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:14.400 --> 01:13:20.960
surge, and the particular pressures on children&nbsp;
and young people’s services. But Julian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:20.960 --> 01:13:24.160
do you want to just give an update on&nbsp;
where we are with the financial settlement?&nbsp;

01:13:25.600 --> 01:13:31.840
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: Yeah, sure. So, I mean,&nbsp;
as we’ll talk about in the planning side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:32.960 --> 01:13:40.720
I mean, recognising the anyway, the commitment and&nbsp;
need to expand mental health services, even before&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:40.720 --> 01:13:47.760
COVID, we will be proceeding with insisting on&nbsp;
everyone meeting the mental investment standard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:47.760 --> 01:13:52.320
we will continue with the planned level&nbsp;
of transformation funding into mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:52.320 --> 01:13:55.520
health. And we’re in the process of just&nbsp;
finalising the details with government&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:57.120 --> 01:13:59.840
for the extra investment in&nbsp;
mental health and NHS workforce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:01.200 --> 01:14:05.440
the results from the 500 million pounds announced&nbsp;
in the spending review, and that is very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:06.080 --> 01:14:12.320
from a mental health service perspective&nbsp;
geared to meeting some of the increased demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:12.320 --> 01:14:16.720
we’ve seen in particular around children&nbsp;
and young people and eating disorders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:17.680 --> 01:14:23.360
as well as accelerating therefore some of the&nbsp;
service expansion we had already planned through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.360 --> 01:14:27.840
the long term plan. And we are just as I said,&nbsp;
in the process of finalising bits of the detail.&nbsp;

01:14:27.840 --> 01:14:34.640
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Julian. Well, can we move&nbsp;
then, Amanda and Pauline, thank you very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:34.640 --> 01:14:41.680
for that. Can we move into the financial position&nbsp;
as of the end of month 10 and then move straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:41.680 --> 01:14:47.680
into the planning guidance, please, Julian.
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: So, assuming board members&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.680 --> 01:14:54.480
have read the paper, it shows very much. I want&nbsp;
to say sort of steady as she goes, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:54.480 --> 01:15:02.240
we will have spent over 12 billion pounds at the&nbsp;
end of month 10, more than the original mandate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:02.240 --> 01:15:10.080
which has funded both, you know, the impact&nbsp;
on core NHS services that result from COVID.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:11.600 --> 01:15:15.680
The much higher, you know, covering the much&nbsp;
higher levels of staff sickness absence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:15.680 --> 01:15:19.680
the additional services that we have&nbsp;
procured from the independent sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:20.720 --> 01:15:25.120
the extra funding into discharge that we&nbsp;
have talked about, extra funding to hospices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:26.240 --> 01:15:31.200
all the necessary steps that we’ve&nbsp;
had to take to deal with COVID.&nbsp;

01:15:31.200 --> 01:15:36.240
But that kind of run rate has been very&nbsp;
steady, clearly in the acute sector&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:36.800 --> 01:15:43.760
as activity has increased through the autumn,&nbsp;
I mean, largely both the recovery of non-COVID,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:43.760 --> 01:15:49.520
non-elective and other elective activity, and&nbsp;
then through December in January, as we dealt with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:50.640 --> 01:15:59.440
the spike in COVID cases in hospital, you’ve seen&nbsp;
a gradual sort of tick up in marginal cost in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:59.440 --> 01:16:10.080
sector but offset by a recovery of other income.
So in aggregate, a very steady spend rate which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:10.080 --> 01:16:18.800
will actually continue through month 11 as well.
And on capital, I mean, clearly, we have put quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:18.800 --> 01:16:24.160
a lot of extra capital in to deal with some&nbsp;
of the critical infrastructure, expansion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:24.800 --> 01:16:31.280
A&amp;E departments pre-winter to deal with some&nbsp;
of the extra demands we saw in the impact of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:31.280 --> 01:16:37.360
social distancing, as well as progress&nbsp;
on eradicating mental health dorms.&nbsp;

01:16:37.360 --> 01:16:42.960
So all of that is in train. I think the kind of&nbsp;
impact of January and February, some of the social&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:42.960 --> 01:16:47.520
distancing, the uptick in COVID will have had an&nbsp;
impact on the delivery of some of those capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:47.520 --> 01:16:56.080
programmes, inevitably. So I suspect we will&nbsp;
slightly underspend at the end, relative to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:56.080 --> 01:17:02.240
allocation made, but you know, but it’s actually&nbsp;
people have largely kept those programmes going.&nbsp;

01:17:04.400 --> 01:17:07.600
Worth saying and we’ll come on to it of&nbsp;
course, we have reached agreement for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:07.600 --> 01:17:12.160
funding for the first half of the year&nbsp;
with government which we can come on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:12.160 --> 01:17:17.280
when we deal with the planning item.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Julian. Does&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:17.280 --> 01:17:21.920
anyone have any questions for Julian&nbsp;
on the current financial results?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:23.920 --> 01:17:29.840
No- I think Julian, we can probably move straight&nbsp;
into the planning guidance for next year, please.&nbsp;

01:17:29.840 --> 01:17:33.920
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: So David, look,&nbsp;
clearly we’re looking for agreement to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:34.560 --> 01:17:43.920
the planning guidance, which we will then publish&nbsp;
after this board if it is agreed out to the NHS.&nbsp;

01:17:43.920 --> 01:17:49.280
Firstly, it’s worth saying the guidance&nbsp;
we’re about to issue has been the result&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:49.280 --> 01:17:57.760
of a lot of consultation and co-creation with&nbsp;
people across the NHS, with national bodies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:57.760 --> 01:18:05.760
for example, in mental health charities or the&nbsp;
work Steve does around cardiovascular disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:06.880 --> 01:18:14.000
So an enormous effort from a lot of people&nbsp;
across the NHS and associated partners.&nbsp;

01:18:16.320 --> 01:18:19.520
Fundamental activities, the funding agreement&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:19.520 --> 01:18:23.360
reached with the government that&nbsp;
builds on the additional funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:23.360 --> 01:18:29.920
announced in the SR for elective recovery and&nbsp;
for mental health and workforce development, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:29.920 --> 01:18:34.800
well as at that point, the additional 1.5 billion&nbsp;
they provided into our kind of baseline funding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:34.800 --> 01:18:40.240
As the Secretary of State announced, we’ve agreed&nbsp;
a further 6.6 billion pounds for the first half&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:41.200 --> 01:18:45.920
of the year, which recognises the extra&nbsp;
costs we carry as a result of COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:46.480 --> 01:18:54.160
and the ongoing kind of constraints we face as a&nbsp;
result of continuing to sort of manage COVID, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:54.160 --> 01:19:02.320
whilst actual demand in particular in the acute&nbsp;
sector has reduced over the last couple of months.&nbsp;

01:19:04.160 --> 01:19:12.800
The plan sets out the steps that we need to take&nbsp;
to recover, particularly elective activity, but in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:12.800 --> 01:19:18.880
other sectors as well through the first half of&nbsp;
the year. The steps that we are going to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:19.920 --> 01:19:25.120
not just to sort of go back to what was done&nbsp;
before but to use this to build on what we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:25.120 --> 01:19:30.160
achieved through COVID in terms of actually&nbsp;
the transformation of outpatients, the much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:30.160 --> 01:19:35.840
greater use of virtual consultations in secondary&nbsp;
and primary care and indeed in mental health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:36.480 --> 01:19:38.960
and some of the other&nbsp;
innovations that we have seen.&nbsp;

01:19:39.920 --> 01:19:46.800
And I mean, what we’ve seen is the sort of need&nbsp;
to double down actually on the investments we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:46.800 --> 01:19:55.760
had already planned to make in things like extra&nbsp;
diagnostic capacity to improve our cancer outcomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:55.760 --> 01:20:00.320
or the expansion in mental health that we’ve just&nbsp;
spoken about, or indeed the extra investment in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:00.320 --> 01:20:04.320
primary and community care so that we’re caring&nbsp;
for people in the kind of optimal setting.&nbsp;

01:20:05.920 --> 01:20:12.000
I think the recovery from COVID both shows&nbsp;
that those things are still necessary and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:12.000 --> 01:20:19.120
will be an even more important part of how&nbsp;
we recover over the next six months and year.&nbsp;

01:20:20.240 --> 01:20:25.280
Whilst the plan largely sets out the priorities&nbsp;
for the first half of the year and the funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:25.280 --> 01:20:31.440
settlement for the first half of the&nbsp;
year, we are giving clear commitments&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:31.440 --> 01:20:37.600
for additional funding into mental health,&nbsp;
primary and community care and some of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:37.600 --> 01:20:43.280
other transformation funding for the&nbsp;
whole year so that we really can get on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:43.280 --> 01:20:48.640
in confidence with the service expansion&nbsp;
we had planned and which is necessary.&nbsp;

01:20:50.720 --> 01:20:57.520
The first item is clearly a core part at the heart&nbsp;
of what we need to do over this next year. It just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:57.520 --> 01:21:04.160
reflects our staff. So the planning guidance&nbsp;
sets out some additional investment into national&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:04.160 --> 01:21:10.080
health and wellbeing services, so a continuation&nbsp;
and expansion of what Prerana’s team has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:10.080 --> 01:21:16.960
successfully put in place over the last 12 months.&nbsp;
It absolutely through the financial arrangements&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:16.960 --> 01:21:25.280
we’ve put in place over the back half of 2021 will&nbsp;
allow organisations to enable staff to carry over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:25.280 --> 01:21:32.880
unused leave and give the flexibility to&nbsp;
staff as to how they choose to take that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:32.880 --> 01:21:36.800
And it allows the continuation and expansion&nbsp;
of some of the workforce supply measures that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:36.800 --> 01:21:42.320
we have put in place through 2021, which I&nbsp;
think will continue to be a critical part&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:42.320 --> 01:21:49.280
of the recovery and plans into 21/22. And we have&nbsp;
worked very closely with Health Education England&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:50.000 --> 01:21:57.040
and their business plan to ensure that kind of&nbsp;
our workforce plans and the kind of future supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:57.040 --> 01:22:01.280
are lined up, because again, that’s going to be a&nbsp;
critical part of the expansion of mental health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:02.480 --> 01:22:10.560
cancer and various other areas. So that’s&nbsp;
absolutely at the heart of what we’re doing.&nbsp;

01:22:10.560 --> 01:22:19.040
Secondly, it sets out the plans for elective&nbsp;
recovery and the incentive system that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:19.040 --> 01:22:28.800
put in place and agreed with government such that&nbsp;
we can pace the recovery of activities sensibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:29.520 --> 01:22:36.320
taking account of staffing needs,&nbsp;
and indeed the need for people to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:38.400 --> 01:22:49.280
you know, actually take some of their annual leave&nbsp;
but frees up systems working at a system level&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:49.280 --> 01:22:56.640
to think, how do they prioritise and put in place&nbsp;
the capacity we are going to need to recover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:56.640 --> 01:23:02.960
you know, some of the growth in waiting lists&nbsp;
that we have had seen and how they can restore&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:03.760 --> 01:23:11.520
activity, as well as where we are expecting to&nbsp;
see further progress in terms of transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:12.640 --> 01:23:18.080
in the way we deal with outpatients, the closer&nbsp;
working between primary and secondary care both&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:18.080 --> 01:23:24.960
on referrals and waiting list management, and then&nbsp;
getting systems to really think how we begin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:25.520 --> 01:23:30.560
transform our clinical pathways to&nbsp;
drive better productivity and better&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:30.560 --> 01:23:33.840
clinical outcomes with the capacity that we have.&nbsp;

01:23:34.800 --> 01:23:41.360
It also clearly sets out cancer as&nbsp;
a major priority for this year, both&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:42.960 --> 01:23:48.080
the restoration of the 62-day standard over the&nbsp;
course of this year, progress in implementing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:48.080 --> 01:23:57.920
the 28 day faster diagnosis standard, as well&nbsp;
as investment to identify quickly those cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:58.640 --> 01:24:04.400
which you know, we have not necessarily, you know,&nbsp;
where we need to catch up on things like screening&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:06.240 --> 01:24:11.440
through the first part of this year, and&nbsp;
we will put an extra 50 million pounds into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:11.440 --> 01:24:17.680
breast screening to that end.
Clearly, as I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:19.600 --> 01:24:26.960
mental health already an absolute priority for&nbsp;
us, more so coming out of COVID. Absolutely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:26.960 --> 01:24:31.680
we will be expecting systems to set&nbsp;
out their plans for the full 12 months&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:33.040 --> 01:24:36.400
for the delivery of the mental health&nbsp;
investment standard. And we will want to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:36.400 --> 01:24:42.560
clearly the funding arrangements for all providers&nbsp;
across those 12 months so that we know that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:42.560 --> 01:24:48.960
funding is going to providers to expand services&nbsp;
in a way we want right at the start of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:48.960 --> 01:24:53.920
backed up with the transformation funding, backed&nbsp;
up with the extra funding the government announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:54.720 --> 01:25:00.160
at the spending review.
We will also be as I said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:00.800 --> 01:25:05.680
setting out the extra investment in goals&nbsp;
for the continued expansion of primary care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:06.240 --> 01:25:14.560
I think we are targeting about 15,000 extra&nbsp;
roles in primary care by the end of 21/22.&nbsp;

01:25:17.440 --> 01:25:23.840
And you know we are sort of&nbsp;
re-establishing normal contractual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:24.960 --> 01:25:30.080
mechanisms and looking at how through&nbsp;
this year we can, in agreement with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:31.520 --> 01:25:36.880
BMA and GPs, introduce the new services&nbsp;
we were already under discussion with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:39.520 --> 01:25:47.200
through contract discussions and looking&nbsp;
for them to prioritise the management of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:47.200 --> 01:25:56.240
long term conditions, build on what we’ve learned&nbsp;
through COVID terms of remote monitoring, virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:56.240 --> 01:26:01.040
consultations and the kind of transformation&nbsp;
we’re expecting to see through digital channels.&nbsp;

01:26:03.120 --> 01:26:09.360
We’ll be putting extra ringfenced investment into&nbsp;
community services. We are going to prioritise&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:09.360 --> 01:26:15.600
investment in the kind of two hour crisis&nbsp;
response, we see all of this as a critical part&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:15.600 --> 01:26:23.200
of, you know, making sure we’re managing people&nbsp;
in the right setting, moderating the pressure on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:26.000 --> 01:26:29.520
emergency departments in the acute sector,&nbsp;
we think that’s going to be critical if we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:29.520 --> 01:26:40.480
going to then recover other backlogs. And,&nbsp;
as we’ve said, maintaining the investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:41.520 --> 01:26:46.240
in the enhanced discharge arrangements through&nbsp;
the first six months, at least the first six&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:46.240 --> 01:26:50.080
months of the year, which we think have&nbsp;
been worth about 6,000 beds in the acute&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:50.080 --> 01:26:57.760
sector and freed up about 10,000 staff. So again,&nbsp;
a really critical part of our recovery programme.&nbsp;

01:26:58.480 --> 01:27:02.480
And as part of helping the emergency&nbsp;
pathway, we will continue to evaluate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:03.520 --> 01:27:08.080
the 111 First arrangements with a view&nbsp;
to expanding them through the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:09.920 --> 01:27:17.840
And we are going to ask for&nbsp;
people to now complete the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:20.320 --> 01:27:28.240
data collection, which will help inform, you know,&nbsp;
the government’s response to the clinical review&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:28.240 --> 01:27:34.400
of standards and the future direction on&nbsp;
the urgent and emergency care pathway.&nbsp;

01:27:35.680 --> 01:27:43.440
So underpinning all of this is going to be a&nbsp;
continued focus on prevention in all of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:44.400 --> 01:27:49.840
settings. We will be putting in extra funding&nbsp;
for weight management, smoking cessation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:52.160 --> 01:27:58.320
tackling issues people have with alcohol through&nbsp;
this year. As I think we spoke about earlier in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:58.320 --> 01:28:04.480
the agenda, we will push a real push on all of&nbsp;
these programmes on health inequalities, a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:04.480 --> 01:28:11.840
improvement in the data that helps us understand&nbsp;
where there are inequalities of access and outcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:11.840 --> 01:28:17.760
by geography, by population group through the&nbsp;
course of this year. And we see that as absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.760 --> 01:28:24.560
the heart of the way we want systems to look at&nbsp;
the sort of population health need, and we’ll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:25.520 --> 01:28:32.560
putting new investment into the way we support&nbsp;
systems in sort of gathering that data.&nbsp;

01:28:35.200 --> 01:28:37.520
Then tied to that is clearly how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:38.240 --> 01:28:45.360
invest in digital transformation,&nbsp;
improving provider digitisation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:45.360 --> 01:28:50.640
but we are through some of the clinical pathways&nbsp;
really exploring how can we transform care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:51.440 --> 01:28:56.400
I mean, really, sort of change it front to back.&nbsp;
And we currently have a real focus on I-care&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:57.440 --> 01:29:06.480
in that and underpinning it or the move to system&nbsp;
working integrated care systems, in which clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:06.480 --> 01:29:11.600
we are anticipating legislation in due course, and&nbsp;
the further development of primary care networks.&nbsp;

01:29:11.600 --> 01:29:16.960
I mean, that’s what the planning guidance sets&nbsp;
out, the funding to back it. And if the board&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:16.960 --> 01:29:22.240
agrees it we will publish it later today.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Julian, that’s very comprehensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:22.800 --> 01:29:29.920
Thank you very much. And Andrew, you’ve seen&nbsp;
many of these planning guidance notes over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:29.920 --> 01:29:34.080
years coming, winging your way when you were at&nbsp;
Frimley and no doubt other hospitals as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:35.040 --> 01:29:41.520
What is your assessment of this?
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: So I think it’s been co-created,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:44.240 --> 01:29:50.720
the other ALBs have been involved. A number&nbsp;
of system leaders and trust leaders have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:50.720 --> 01:29:55.840
been involved. So that’s really positive.
I think it’s very clear in its messaging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:55.840 --> 01:30:04.480
it sets out some very clear priorities. So I&nbsp;
think the service will have the clarity it needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:05.040 --> 01:30:11.680
to deliver some of the big challenges that it’s&nbsp;
got ahead of it and capacity is going to probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:11.680 --> 01:30:20.160
be, and workforce, the two must sorts that we’re&nbsp;
going to have to tackle and I’m more worried&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:20.160 --> 01:30:30.080
about the capacity issue than I am the workforce.
And I think if we’re not careful, we will see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:30.080 --> 01:30:37.600
dent in our ability to you know, really tackle the&nbsp;
backlog in terms of waiting lists, particularly if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:38.800 --> 01:30:44.560
social care is under the cosh financially in&nbsp;
the back half of the coming financial year.&nbsp;

01:30:45.120 --> 01:30:54.560
So hopefully as Simon negotiates the settlement&nbsp;
and Julian for the last six months of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:54.560 --> 01:30:59.760
you know, we can continue with the scheme that&nbsp;
Ian has got, discharge to assess schemes. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:59.760 --> 01:31:05.840
it’s so important given the, you know, the massive&nbsp;
beds that it’s freed up for the service, and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:05.840 --> 01:31:13.440
need every bed that we can free up to handle the&nbsp;
elective backlog. So I think the document reads&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:13.440 --> 01:31:19.760
really well. It’s very clear, most chief execs,&nbsp;
I think, will be really supportive of this. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:19.760 --> 01:31:26.640
it sets the template, the must do template for the&nbsp;
coming financial year. So I think it’s very clear.&nbsp;

01:31:26.640 --> 01:31:30.960
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Andrew. And&nbsp;
Patrick, particularly focusing on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:30.960 --> 01:31:38.160
incentives that are built into the plans to&nbsp;
reduce the, to attack the elective backlog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:38.160 --> 01:31:42.640
do you think that gets about the right balance?
&gt;&gt;Patrick Carter of Coles: Yeah, I think it does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:42.640 --> 01:31:48.720
David. And I think the two step approach, ramping&nbsp;
it up gradually, recognising we just can’t sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:48.720 --> 01:31:54.640
turn the tap on immediately, but we certainly&nbsp;
need to keep focused on these incentives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:54.640 --> 01:32:00.000
because we do need to get all providers&nbsp;
really cranked up as we come out of this.&nbsp;

01:32:00.800 --> 01:32:05.040
As Andrew just said, the backlog is&nbsp;
going to be the big challenge we face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:05.840 --> 01:32:12.160
And we’re going to have to do everything we&nbsp;
can to organise to overcome that. So I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:12.160 --> 01:32:18.240
this is one of the best documents I have seen in&nbsp;
this. And I think it’s a great way forward. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:19.200 --> 01:32:23.040
the most important thing is we’ve got the&nbsp;
money to deliver it, so really important.&nbsp;

01:32:23.040 --> 01:32:28.160
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think the narrative&nbsp;
feels right to me, Julian. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:28.160 --> 01:32:34.320
it is much more moving towards systems, there’s a&nbsp;
big emphasis on health inequalities. I think it’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:34.320 --> 01:32:39.760
I mean, I think it’s a terrifically high quality&nbsp;
piece of work to be honest with you. So look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:39.760 --> 01:32:45.760
could you thank all your team, I know if it&nbsp;
hadn’t been, you’d have got the blame for it, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:45.760 --> 01:32:51.120
we will spread the praise around. But before we&nbsp;
do that, I better just ask the board whether they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:51.120 --> 01:32:56.400
are going to approve it or not. Can I assume that&nbsp;
unless anyone says otherwise, that we’re in favour&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:56.400 --> 01:33:00.640
of this? And it’ll be published later on today?&nbsp;
I think it’s a really first-class piece of work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:01.200 --> 01:33:06.240
Julian and Amanda, thank you very much.
So can we then move on to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:06.800 --> 01:33:11.040
system framework, then, Amanda?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:11.040 --> 01:33:18.400
much. And so similar request to the board, which&nbsp;
is to approve this to go out to consultation. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:18.400 --> 01:33:26.320
slightly different process around this. But what&nbsp;
I wanted to do was just draw attention, if I may,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:26.320 --> 01:33:30.240
to the kind of two big things that have changed.&nbsp;
And I’ll just say one or two other things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:30.880 --> 01:33:37.040
rather than get into a lot of detail.
First thing to say is the big change is this now&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:37.040 --> 01:33:47.200
reflects a single framework that brings together&nbsp;
providers, CCGs, and systems into the same single&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:47.200 --> 01:33:52.800
oversight framework to build on where we were&nbsp;
before, which had done a similar job for providers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:52.800 --> 01:33:59.200
and CCGs, but this adds the system lens. Second&nbsp;
big change is that it is proposing replacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:59.200 --> 01:34:06.160
the separate provider-focused quality special&nbsp;
measures and financial special measures regime&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:06.160 --> 01:34:13.040
with a new recovery support programme focused&nbsp;
on individual organisations and/or systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:13.040 --> 01:34:17.840
but taking a much more integrated approach.
And we have developed this, thank you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:17.840 --> 01:34:23.680
for those of you who’ve been involved with us.&nbsp;
But we have developed this also with NHS leaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:23.680 --> 01:34:30.240
stakeholders, including the CQC and with system&nbsp;
leaders. And this does also reflect just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:30.240 --> 01:34:36.720
the earlier conversation, an increasing role&nbsp;
for ICSs in the oversight process, linked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:36.720 --> 01:34:43.120
course to their maturity and development journey.
So just a few other things I wanted to call out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:44.000 --> 01:34:52.320
This framework proposes a single national&nbsp;
scorecard looking at five sort of oversight&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:52.320 --> 01:34:59.200
themes which are aligned to the long term plan.&nbsp;
The metrics will be drawn based on the 21/22&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:00.560 --> 01:35:04.320
planning priorities and links exactly&nbsp;
to what Julian’s just taken us through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:05.040 --> 01:35:10.400
But the thing we’re also proposing is that there&nbsp;
would be space in this framework for a sixth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:10.400 --> 01:35:16.160
theme, and that will be determined locally. So&nbsp;
finding the balance between consistent national&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:16.160 --> 01:35:21.920
framework but appropriate local flexibility&nbsp;
to reflect local population priorities.&nbsp;

01:35:22.960 --> 01:35:27.600
Next thing I just wanted to mention&nbsp;
was about the segmentation approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:27.600 --> 01:35:31.920
So this, again, is a continuation of something&nbsp;
the NHS has had in place for a long time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:32.640 --> 01:35:37.360
But it does move us on I think, from something&nbsp;
that perhaps felt like it was a little bit of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:37.360 --> 01:35:43.760
cliff edge whether you were in or out of special&nbsp;
measures to something that offers a much clearer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:43.760 --> 01:35:51.840
definition of support. If you find yourself&nbsp;
in segment three, targeted support based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:52.480 --> 01:35:57.040
the best practice that we have access to&nbsp;
within the NHS and our ability to call on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:58.000 --> 01:36:02.960
colleagues for example in the GIRFT team&nbsp;
who can come and help do some of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:04.320 --> 01:36:11.680
pathway redesign and clinically led transformation&nbsp;
work, or indeed specialist support around things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:11.680 --> 01:36:18.080
like urgent emergency care, for example.
And then segment four becomes the mandated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:18.880 --> 01:36:24.080
recovery support programme, but with much less,&nbsp;
as I say, a kind of cliff edge between the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:24.080 --> 01:36:30.480
and much more of a sense of being able to call&nbsp;
on consistent support offer for all organisations&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:30.480 --> 01:36:35.520
no matter where they are, but as I say, with a&nbsp;
targeted support offer, segment three, a mandated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:35.520 --> 01:36:41.200
recovery support programme, segment four.
And final thing, David, was just to say the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:41.200 --> 01:36:47.280
proposal at the moment is, if we approve today,&nbsp;
we will go out immediately for consultation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:47.280 --> 01:36:53.600
six weeks, with a view then to implementation,&nbsp;
but in a phased way this year, to reflect the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:53.600 --> 01:37:01.040
uncertainties that we have in light of COVID. And&nbsp;
with an initial focus really on getting if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:01.040 --> 01:37:05.520
like the sort of segments three and four sorted&nbsp;
out so that we can make sure that we’re offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:05.520 --> 01:37:14.720
support in an appropriate and targeted way.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Amanda. Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:14.720 --> 01:37:23.520
I mean, you’ll see running through this, a big&nbsp;
focus on population health and on reducing health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:23.520 --> 01:37:28.880
inequalities, I mean, from your own experience,&nbsp;
do you think we’ll get the framework about right?&nbsp;

01:37:30.080 --> 01:37:36.880
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: I do. I think it’s terrific. I&nbsp;
think that direction of travel here is excellent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:36.880 --> 01:37:44.160
of course, lots of work ahead, but it’s&nbsp;
exciting work. And I might frame this as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:46.800 --> 01:37:52.240
adding one thing to what Emily&nbsp;
Lawson said about the advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:52.960 --> 01:37:57.280
that the NHS provided in providing&nbsp;
such extraordinary vaccine coverage.&nbsp;

01:37:58.640 --> 01:38:05.360
In the world that I live in, I am so envious of&nbsp;
this and so looking forward to living in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:05.360 --> 01:38:14.960
world, and that is end to end control of delivery.&nbsp;
That is something that doesn’t exist in the US.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:15.680 --> 01:38:26.720
And in an age in which we try to use particularly&nbsp;
digital technology to improve service delivery&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:27.360 --> 01:38:32.480
and the generation of data for management,&nbsp;
having a view of the entire process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:33.200 --> 01:38:41.760
is a huge advantage. And so I see the coming&nbsp;
together of the funding, the ICSs, the team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:43.520 --> 01:38:54.080
as has been discussed the sense of team with this&nbsp;
view toward the end-to-end process as enabling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:54.960 --> 01:39:05.200
local work on improvement that is the only way to&nbsp;
scale. Right? Because you can’t scale with central&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:05.200 --> 01:39:14.688
authority. It has to be through local improvement.&nbsp;
So I’m, I hope that was on point, David. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:16.320 --> 01:39:21.680
my assessment from this side of the pond.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Tim. And Laura,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:22.800 --> 01:39:30.240
Laura Wade-Gery that is, from a digital point of&nbsp;
view, do you feel that the digital transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:30.240 --> 01:39:35.840
opportunities, and the opportunities of using&nbsp;
data are sufficiently reflected in this?&nbsp;

01:39:35.840 --> 01:39:44.560
&gt;&gt;Laura Wade-Gery: I think, David, I think&nbsp;
yes, would be the short answer. I suspect that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:45.120 --> 01:39:54.560
we’re still on that journey about always embedding&nbsp;
it. But I think I’d really call out Tim’s point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:54.560 --> 01:40:00.160
around, what we’ve been very clear about I&nbsp;
would say, the exec been very clear about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:00.160 --> 01:40:06.320
is the importance of data to enable population&nbsp;
health management and how we ensure that as ICSs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:06.320 --> 01:40:13.760
come together, that is the that is one of the key&nbsp;
foundational blocks. So I think that we are, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:13.760 --> 01:40:20.720
seems to me absolutely key to the approach here.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Laura. So unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:20.720 --> 01:40:27.760
there are any questions, other questions for&nbsp;
Amanda, can I assume the board is happy that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:27.760 --> 01:40:34.400
this should go out to consultation in the way that&nbsp;
it’s been expressed in this paper? Yeah? Amanda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:34.400 --> 01:40:39.920
that’s great. Thank you very much indeed.
I mean, Simon, you must reflect back to when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:39.920 --> 01:40:46.880
first wrote your five year forward view, what, six&nbsp;
or seven years ago now, when you were just sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:46.880 --> 01:40:52.560
positing this as a potential endpoint, I think.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:52.560 --> 01:40:59.280
Yeah, I think the point is that the clock&nbsp;
speed of integration or joining up services&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:59.280 --> 01:41:04.160
has dramatically improved over the last 12 months&nbsp;
through the pandemic as we’ve talked about, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:04.960 --> 01:41:10.720
we will have got, you know, nine tenths&nbsp;
of the way there in organisational terms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:11.600 --> 01:41:15.280
The final one-tenth is what we need the&nbsp;
legislation for, that we’re expecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:15.280 --> 01:41:18.720
the government to bring forward during the&nbsp;
course of this next parliamentary session.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:19.600 --> 01:41:23.920
But from the other end of the telescope, it’s&nbsp;
all about the patient experience. And it’s about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:23.920 --> 01:41:28.880
using what we’ve learned around the vaccine&nbsp;
programme to in real time design out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:30.560 --> 01:41:34.560
inequalities in access and uptake&nbsp;
and doing that for cancer screening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:34.560 --> 01:41:38.720
for urgent cardiovascular care, for young&nbsp;
people’s mental health services, and making&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:38.720 --> 01:41:42.640
that the new way in which the health&nbsp;
service works. And I think people have now&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:43.680 --> 01:41:48.000
lived that, even over the last several months on&nbsp;
the back of COVID vaccine, and can see that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:48.000 --> 01:41:52.480
is the way we want to operate the service as a&nbsp;
whole. And so all of this talk about, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:52.480 --> 01:41:56.240
integrated care systems and governance and so&nbsp;
forth, all of that is just a means to an end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:56.800 --> 01:42:01.520
Where the magic actually happens, which&nbsp;
is in the encounters between patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:02.160 --> 01:42:07.120
the public and the health service locally.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good, thanks very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:07.120 --> 01:42:14.880
Simon. So Prerana, over to you.
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Thank you, David. And you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:14.880 --> 01:42:20.640
many of my colleagues have mentioned supporting&nbsp;
staff already so I’ll build on what people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:20.640 --> 01:42:27.200
already shared. So first of all, a huge thank you&nbsp;
from me on behalf of the entire executive team, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:27.200 --> 01:42:34.080
everyone who has worked so hard and responded in&nbsp;
such an extraordinary way during this past year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:34.080 --> 01:42:43.520
The scale of response has meant that staff are&nbsp;
tired, but also will share the examples of having&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:43.520 --> 01:42:50.960
transformed the way they are providing care, of&nbsp;
teams coming together, of different roles, as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:50.960 --> 01:42:57.360
discussed before, contributing to this response.
So our focus now is on supporting staff to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:57.360 --> 01:43:02.000
recover. And before I go into some of the key&nbsp;
elements were focusing on I wanted to recount&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:02.000 --> 01:43:08.160
a conversation I had with a medic at the&nbsp;
Swaminarayan temple vaccination centre.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:08.720 --> 01:43:13.520
And, you know, we were both wearing masks, and&nbsp;
we couldn’t really see each other’s faces. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:13.520 --> 01:43:18.960
asked Sapna, because she’s a medic in a critical&nbsp;
care unit in London, how she was doing and she&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:18.960 --> 01:43:26.080
said, actually, I’m really tired from my week, but&nbsp;
it was a Saturday and Sapna was vaccinating and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:26.080 --> 01:43:30.080
asked her, well, shouldn’t you be resting then&nbsp;
if you’re really tired, and she said, actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:30.720 --> 01:43:36.400
this is giving me so much energy. I’m going to&nbsp;
be here on Sunday as well, because the COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:36.400 --> 01:43:43.680
vaccine and our efforts as an NHS to vaccinate&nbsp;
the entire population is our way out of this year.&nbsp;

01:43:43.680 --> 01:43:50.400
And so I think her experience of both feeling&nbsp;
tired with the year but also feeling extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:50.400 --> 01:43:58.080
hopeful and determined to see the next steps&nbsp;
through and help reach patients who are waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:58.080 --> 01:44:03.760
and look at activity recovery would probably&nbsp;
represent a lot of people’s experiences.&nbsp;

01:44:04.560 --> 01:44:10.800
So I was struck by that conversation.&nbsp;
And so what I wanted to share is that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:10.800 --> 01:44:17.120
in March of 2020, we had along with local offers&nbsp;
to support staff, put in place a national health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:17.120 --> 01:44:22.880
and wellbeing offer that Amanda had referred&nbsp;
to, which has now been accessed 780,000 times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:23.920 --> 01:44:30.640
which shows that people are focusing on accessing&nbsp;
care. We had convened and NHSEI had convened an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:30.640 --> 01:44:36.880
expert advisory group in March of last year that&nbsp;
has been advising us on how we can support staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:36.880 --> 01:44:42.800
on an ongoing basis. And every month in the&nbsp;
last 12 months, we have added something to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:42.800 --> 01:44:48.240
national offer based on the feedback of staff.
So for instance, we added a bereavement&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:48.240 --> 01:44:53.120
counselling service, we added a financial&nbsp;
advice service because people had shared that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:53.120 --> 01:44:56.640
they had secondary stressors in terms&nbsp;
of what was happening with families.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:57.920 --> 01:45:04.400
We added a bespoke bereavement service&nbsp;
in different languages. We of course have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:04.400 --> 01:45:10.800
lots of health and wellbeing apps that&nbsp;
have been downloaded by more than 150,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:10.800 --> 01:45:18.320
people. So we’ve built in listening into the&nbsp;
process. We have had pulse surveys every month.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:18.320 --> 01:45:26.240
And with that we are constantly expanding and&nbsp;
fine tuning the offers from NHSEI. But also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:26.800 --> 01:45:32.480
I know that employers have really, you know, made&nbsp;
sure that they are supporting their staff as well.&nbsp;

01:45:32.480 --> 01:45:38.400
And the staff survey results for 2020 confirm&nbsp;
this. So the staff have said that there’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:38.400 --> 01:45:43.120
greater focus on their health and wellbeing&nbsp;
during the pandemic. And that theme has shown the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:43.120 --> 01:45:49.840
greatest improvement across all of the themes in&nbsp;
the staff survey. There’s a 4.1 percentage point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:49.840 --> 01:45:54.800
increase in staff feeling that their organisation&nbsp;
takes positive action on health and wellbeing.&nbsp;

01:45:56.000 --> 01:46:01.920
And I do believe that this will be one of the&nbsp;
you know, one of the enduring legacies of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:01.920 --> 01:46:08.640
pandemic, that we have ongoing support for&nbsp;
staff. However, there was also an increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:08.640 --> 01:46:14.320
of 3.6 percentage points in those staff&nbsp;
who say they have felt unwell as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:14.320 --> 01:46:18.320
of work-related stress within the past 12&nbsp;
months, that’s in the staff survey as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:18.320 --> 01:46:23.840
And with the worldwide pandemic and the&nbsp;
pressure on health services around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:23.840 --> 01:46:32.800
it’s no surprise that our staff have also had to&nbsp;
respond in in this way and feel tired as a result.&nbsp;

01:46:32.800 --> 01:46:36.560
So therefore, what are some of the principles&nbsp;
of recovery, of wellbeing recovery that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:36.560 --> 01:46:41.760
have been working on and some of the actions&nbsp;
that are already in train and more to follow?&nbsp;

01:46:41.760 --> 01:46:48.480
Our expert advisory group has said, recovery&nbsp;
needs to be individual for each person in the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:48.480 --> 01:46:54.080
so that we can make resources available for&nbsp;
the vast majority of people who would like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:54.080 --> 01:47:00.400
to access those resources as and when needed. Our&nbsp;
experts have also said we should be careful not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:00.400 --> 01:47:07.520
to over-medicalise the support that we provide.&nbsp;
So we are looking, you know, we’re going to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:07.520 --> 01:47:15.200
guidance and resources available for individuals,&nbsp;
for teams to reflect on the last year or process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:15.200 --> 01:47:20.160
the year but also then look at the lessons&nbsp;
learned, the innovations that have taken place in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:20.160 --> 01:47:25.600
the last year, and then look towards the future.
You know, some of the things that people have said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:25.600 --> 01:47:31.600
they have really appreciated and would like to&nbsp;
take forward are flexibility in ways of working,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:31.600 --> 01:47:37.120
reduction of bureaucracy, enhanced&nbsp;
teamwork, and expanding some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:37.120 --> 01:47:40.880
skills that they’ve had opportunities to&nbsp;
work in other teams and other functions.&nbsp;

01:47:42.080 --> 01:47:47.440
So we want to make sure that we are providing that&nbsp;
support to teams and employers as well, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:48.320 --> 01:47:53.840
research shows that our wellbeing depends on&nbsp;
what is happening in our workplace every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:53.840 --> 01:48:00.560
and how our teams are coming together.
So what we’re doing is making sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:00.560 --> 01:48:06.400
we are providing this guidance&nbsp;
to all employers and supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:06.400 --> 01:48:13.520
with the national health and wellbeing offer.
What we’re also looking at is the vaccination&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:13.520 --> 01:48:19.040
programme being part and parcel of this&nbsp;
wellbeing offer, which means the first dose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:19.040 --> 01:48:25.200
staff protection issue, and a patient protection&nbsp;
issue, the second dose, the flu vaccination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:25.200 --> 01:48:31.360
and so on. As Steve mentioned, we don’t know&nbsp;
what the COVID vaccine programme and protocol&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:31.360 --> 01:48:37.200
is going to look like so we would like to make&nbsp;
sure that staff stay protected in this process.&nbsp;

01:48:37.760 --> 01:48:44.480
What we’re also doing is making support available&nbsp;
to leaders and managers. We’ve had 20,000 leaders&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:44.480 --> 01:48:51.120
attend regionally led workshops, we have an&nbsp;
offering of an executive suite for leaders&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:51.120 --> 01:48:55.600
for the COVID response especially and&nbsp;
we’re going to expand that for recovery.&nbsp;

01:48:55.600 --> 01:49:03.200
We have something called Project M, which&nbsp;
is aimed at managers. We know that managers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:03.200 --> 01:49:08.960
and leaders have also had to shoulder new&nbsp;
responsibilities and new challenges. So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:08.960 --> 01:49:19.360
have very dedicated support available for them.
We have also set up a new role at the board level&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:19.360 --> 01:49:25.680
in the provider sector, which is a wellbeing&nbsp;
guardian. And that role has been taken up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:26.240 --> 01:49:32.880
by lots of organisations. And in our launch event,&nbsp;
we had two large events actually, we’ve had 450&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:32.880 --> 01:49:39.280
net attendees delegates, and the reason is that&nbsp;
we want health and wellbeing of our staff to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:39.280 --> 01:49:44.720
a board level responsibility. And I’m really&nbsp;
heartened by the kind of interest we’ve had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:44.720 --> 01:49:52.880
from basically 450 delegates at the board level&nbsp;
attending and many having signed up for this role.&nbsp;

01:49:54.160 --> 01:49:58.480
We are also saying that every staff member&nbsp;
should have a health and wellbeing conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:58.480 --> 01:50:03.760
in the in the first six months of the year&nbsp;
and then regularly going forward. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:03.760 --> 01:50:11.680
because with the learning from the risk assessment&nbsp;
conversations we’ve had in the summer of last year&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:11.680 --> 01:50:17.920
to keep staff safe, what we see is that we need&nbsp;
this conversation to be regular, annual and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:17.920 --> 01:50:23.440
every single staff member so that staff can talk&nbsp;
to their line managers about what they need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:23.440 --> 01:50:30.320
be able to do their work and job well. So it is&nbsp;
something that we are going to be focusing on.&nbsp;

01:50:31.200 --> 01:50:37.920
As Amanda mentioned, 15 million has been&nbsp;
invested to establish 40 mental health hubs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:37.920 --> 01:50:44.400
one for each system. 22 hubs are now live,&nbsp;
which means that staff are able to access them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:44.400 --> 01:50:51.360
there is rapid self-referral, there is access&nbsp;
to specialised mental health support. And that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:51.360 --> 01:50:56.560
really, really important because there is a group,&nbsp;
there are a group of staff who are of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:56.560 --> 01:51:00.800
in need of more specialised support and we want&nbsp;
to make sure that they are able to access it.&nbsp;

01:51:01.840 --> 01:51:04.960
I think this was mentioned already&nbsp;
that the impact of the pandemic on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:05.680 --> 01:51:13.520
critical care staff has been extensive as well. So&nbsp;
the hubs, the occupational health hubs and mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:13.520 --> 01:51:19.280
health hubs, are doing proactive outreach to&nbsp;
critical care teams for support. And that’s really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:19.280 --> 01:51:24.400
important. So we’re not waiting for staff to come&nbsp;
to us, we are reaching out to ask when support is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:24.400 --> 01:51:30.080
needed. And there was a recent online briefing&nbsp;
with over 250 clinical leaders participating.&nbsp;

01:51:30.720 --> 01:51:35.680
In addition, 400 critical care nursing staff&nbsp;
are registered to be trained as facilitators&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:35.680 --> 01:51:40.800
of restorative supervision to their own&nbsp;
colleagues. And the first training cohort started&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:40.800 --> 01:51:46.960
on 3rd March. This is a very new and innovative&nbsp;
approach and this has been advised by our expert&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:47.520 --> 01:51:52.880
advisory group, which includes psychiatrists,&nbsp;
psychologists, experts from the military, etc, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:52.880 --> 01:51:58.560
have said that restorative supervision after this&nbsp;
kind of year is extremely important for recovery.&nbsp;

01:52:00.000 --> 01:52:04.640
And then, the last thing I will say is that the&nbsp;
impact of this work of the health and wellbeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:04.640 --> 01:52:10.400
support is going to be evaluated on an ongoing&nbsp;
basis through the boards with the wellbeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:10.400 --> 01:52:16.080
guardian playing an important role, as well as&nbsp;
we’ve had one independent evaluation already last&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:16.080 --> 01:52:23.680
year, and we will continue with that as well. So&nbsp;
let me stop here and very happy to take questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:23.680 --> 01:52:30.320
and, and, you know, have a conversation. But&nbsp;
again, my thanks to all staff in the NHS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:30.320 --> 01:52:35.280
And also, I would like to tell them that research&nbsp;
says healthcare professionals are amongst the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:35.280 --> 01:52:40.320
to seek help. And I would just encourage everybody&nbsp;
that there are resources available for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:40.320 --> 01:52:44.080
please do access them. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks Prerana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:44.080 --> 01:52:50.480
well, that’s a very, very important message for&nbsp;
you to finish up on. Any questions for Prerana?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:50.480 --> 01:52:57.840
I wonder whether David Behan would like to&nbsp;
come in here, and then I’ll come to Wol.&nbsp;

01:52:57.840 --> 01:53:03.360
&gt;&gt;David Behan: Not a question, David, just to&nbsp;
support what Prerana has set out. We have put in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:03.360 --> 01:53:09.760
place the people board and the architecture around&nbsp;
that, which is one of the ways to bring all key&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:09.760 --> 01:53:15.680
stakeholders and partners together to support this&nbsp;
work. And I think this emphasis has run through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:15.680 --> 01:53:22.240
today’s board meeting really, about collaboration&nbsp;
at a national, regional, system and local level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:22.240 --> 01:53:26.960
is the one that I’d emphasise around&nbsp;
workforce, whether it’s a workforce supply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:26.960 --> 01:53:31.280
or whether it’s supporting the workforce&nbsp;
that we’ve got in terms of retention and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:31.920 --> 01:53:36.800
some of the measures that Prerana has outlined.
I think that adage that we’re stronger together&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:36.800 --> 01:53:42.320
has never been truer, to be honest. And I&nbsp;
think it is essential that we work together and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:43.840 --> 01:53:50.880
Prerana and Davina have been working together&nbsp;
on chairing the people board and Andrew Morris&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:50.880 --> 01:53:57.200
and myself and Lee McDonough in the DH&nbsp;
provide that sponsorship support to that.&nbsp;

01:53:57.760 --> 01:54:01.680
We had a very good stakeholder meeting last week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:01.680 --> 01:54:05.760
I think it was, which brought together I&nbsp;
think 100 people on the call representing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:06.640 --> 01:54:13.680
these key stakeholders. It’s such a complex&nbsp;
landscape, David, and creating the preconditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:13.680 --> 01:54:18.800
for success, in my view means that we’ve got to&nbsp;
get this collaboration at a national level. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:18.800 --> 01:54:25.120
I think what Prerana has outlined is absolutely to&nbsp;
the point. And just to echo what Julian said when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:25.120 --> 01:54:29.760
he was introducing the planning guidance about&nbsp;
just how important is we work together to secure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:31.120 --> 01:54:36.160
the workforce for the future, but also&nbsp;
support the workforce of today. Thanks.&nbsp;

01:54:38.080 --> 01:54:45.840
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, David. Wol?
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thanks, David. Prerana, great paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:45.840 --> 01:54:52.560
lots of very important issues been brought&nbsp;
up. And lots of interesting interventions.&nbsp;

01:54:52.560 --> 01:54:57.520
I think, for me, I’m just going to&nbsp;
look forward and frame this by saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:58.320 --> 01:55:04.400
I think anyone in business running any sort&nbsp;
of organisation recognises the draining of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:04.400 --> 01:55:08.320
social capital that’s occurred over the&nbsp;
last 12 months. And that’s partly through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:08.320 --> 01:55:12.720
not just asking people to work harder and more&nbsp;
innovatively and change and embrace change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:13.360 --> 01:55:18.240
but it’s also to do with the fact that they’re&nbsp;
working in unfamiliar environments. And I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:18.240 --> 01:55:21.920
people say working from home is great, but&nbsp;
it’s an unfamiliar environment to work in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:21.920 --> 01:55:26.880
We’re now going to try and recover and come back&nbsp;
to whatever the normality is that we come back to.&nbsp;

01:55:28.000 --> 01:55:34.560
And I just want to make sure Prerana that in your&nbsp;
in your thinking, this is a permanent hardwired&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:35.280 --> 01:55:41.040
activity. Because to recover the social&nbsp;
capital we’ve drained, a whole range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:41.040 --> 01:55:44.240
things some of which David touched on, and&nbsp;
other people touched on to this board meeting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:44.880 --> 01:55:51.040
but this health and wellbeing as a point&nbsp;
to focus on, will need to keep going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:51.040 --> 01:55:56.080
and redoubling efforts to make sure across the&nbsp;
whole system, people constantly think about this.&nbsp;

01:55:56.080 --> 01:56:00.240
Because the danger is, we go back into normality,&nbsp;
a year out, two years out, we forget about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:00.240 --> 01:56:04.880
or it gets downgraded and downgraded every&nbsp;
year. So for me, the question to you is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:04.880 --> 01:56:08.640
what can we do to hardwire this in? And&nbsp;
by the way, that’s an invitation to ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:09.360 --> 01:56:16.800
the board to be held accountable for this?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Do you want to respond to that?&nbsp;

01:56:16.800 --> 01:56:22.160
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Yes. Absolutely. Thank you.&nbsp;
Thank you first to David, David Behan, for your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:22.720 --> 01:56:27.280
painting of that picture of collaboration,&nbsp;
which is absolutely right. And, you know, is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:29.600 --> 01:56:34.960
I was going to say, felt reality, lived&nbsp;
reality or felt reality. And it has really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:35.520 --> 01:56:41.040
I think the acceleration in collaboration over&nbsp;
the last year has been phenomenal. And we are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:41.040 --> 01:56:47.120
going to be focused on keeping it that way.
Wol, you are absolutely right, and how I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:47.120 --> 01:56:53.200
thinking about it, and how I’m thinking about it&nbsp;
with leaders, is that from a programme approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:53.200 --> 01:56:59.120
so health and wellbeing sort of as a programme,&nbsp;
to making it the way people manage and lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:59.120 --> 01:57:06.000
and the way people come to work in the NHS, that’s&nbsp;
the arc that we are focusing on. So the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:06.000 --> 01:57:10.160
inputs we’re putting into the line manager&nbsp;
programmes, training programmes, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:10.160 --> 01:57:15.200
the leadership inputs, it has to be&nbsp;
the way managers lead and the way teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:15.200 --> 01:57:20.640
come together, it has to be the way we work.
So what we don’t want it to be is the programme&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:20.640 --> 01:57:26.640
for too long, because then as you say,&nbsp;
it can slip by the wayside. So of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:26.640 --> 01:57:32.800
the resources will be available, but it has to&nbsp;
become a manager’s role to make sure that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:32.800 --> 01:57:38.000
staff are well. How do we design for that?&nbsp;
How do we train for that? How do we recruit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:38.000 --> 01:57:42.880
for that and assess for that? So that’s really&nbsp;
the focus of actually the recovery and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:42.880 --> 01:57:47.840
planning guidance that Julian has outlined.
The first priority is staff wellbeing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:47.840 --> 01:57:53.040
recovery, and I think that’s such an important&nbsp;
signal to everybody in the service. And then as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:53.040 --> 01:57:57.680
David mentioned, Davina and I are co-chairing&nbsp;
the national people board, where we’re looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:57.680 --> 01:58:03.920
at the people plan 22 and beyond, and that this&nbsp;
is actually one of the big focus areas for us&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:03.920 --> 01:58:08.800
for that as well. So just to underline&nbsp;
and reassure that it’s very much part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:08.800 --> 01:58:13.680
our longer-term focus, but I would, you know,&nbsp;
it would be great for us to have a continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:13.680 --> 01:58:18.160
dialogue about this as well. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think, Prerana, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:18.160 --> 01:58:23.280
Amanda made it very clear discussing the&nbsp;
operating model earlier, that the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:23.280 --> 01:58:30.160
and culture are fundamental to delivering the&nbsp;
whole programme. I mean, it’s, it’s so important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:31.040 --> 01:58:34.800
Could you just touch a little&nbsp;
bit on the work you’re doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:35.760 --> 01:58:44.720
the revitalisation that you’re done with the RES,&nbsp;
and also the results of encouraging people from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:44.720 --> 01:58:52.000
BAME backgrounds into more senior jobs in the NHS?
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Thank you, David. So the paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:52.000 --> 01:58:59.520
that’s part of the board papers on the NHS England&nbsp;
and Improvement 19% goal progress, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:02.320 --> 01:59:10.560
Simon made a commitment a year ago, to represent,&nbsp;
to have 19% representation of BAME colleagues at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:10.560 --> 01:59:17.280
all levels of NHSEI in the next five years.&nbsp;
And this is a very, it’s actually a really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:18.480 --> 01:59:23.840
you know, ambitious aspiration goal, rightly so.
In other parts of the NHS, the RES goals are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:23.840 --> 01:59:29.280
over a 10-year period. And it’s right&nbsp;
that as the as the arms-length body&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:29.280 --> 01:59:35.520
that the NHS England and Improvement has taken&nbsp;
on a more aspirational goal of five years.&nbsp;

01:59:35.520 --> 01:59:41.120
So we started last year to look at what are some&nbsp;
of the interventions that we need to do to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:41.120 --> 01:59:49.040
away some of the barriers to that goal, as well&nbsp;
as support all colleagues to make this a reality.&nbsp;

01:59:49.040 --> 01:59:53.760
So I just wanted to point out a couple of&nbsp;
things that we’ve seen in the last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:53.760 --> 01:59:58.560
one that focus on data, and having&nbsp;
transparency across leaders has really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:58.560 --> 02:00:02.640
helped us to make sure we’re holding ourselves&nbsp;
accountable for moving towards this goal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:03.520 --> 02:00:10.000
We’ve done that through overhauling recruitment&nbsp;
practices using data and analytics. And as I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:10.000 --> 02:00:14.560
leaders taking real ownership for the&nbsp;
outcome. And that has been a key focus.&nbsp;

02:00:15.200 --> 02:00:22.080
Also not having lots of different things for us&nbsp;
to do, but really being, you know, prioritising&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:22.080 --> 02:00:26.480
this aspect. And that’s been the experience of&nbsp;
other leaders in the service as well, that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:26.480 --> 02:00:32.240
we focus on one particular ambitious goal, you&nbsp;
know, there’s more, there’s more progress made.&nbsp;

02:00:32.800 --> 02:00:39.040
So we’ve also had a couple of other things we’ve&nbsp;
done. Full transparency of all assignments that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:39.040 --> 02:00:42.720
have been available. So whether it’s,&nbsp;
you know, opportunities to step up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:42.720 --> 02:00:47.280
different projects, and of course, in the last&nbsp;
year, there have been so many opportunities for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:47.280 --> 02:00:50.800
colleagues to do that. So there’s been&nbsp;
transparency of all those assignments.&nbsp;

02:00:51.760 --> 02:00:57.280
We have conducted talent conversations with all&nbsp;
BAME colleagues in NHS England and Improvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:57.280 --> 02:01:01.680
between them and line managers, and this&nbsp;
will be expanded to all staff this year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:02.880 --> 02:01:08.000
We’ve been making changes on multiple fronts:&nbsp;
policy, practice and behaviour, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:08.000 --> 02:01:15.520
leadership behaviour. So what we’ve seen is&nbsp;
that in the very senior grades, so ESM level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:15.520 --> 02:01:22.800
there’s been a 3.5% increase in BAME staff&nbsp;
over the last 12 months. And at band nine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:22.800 --> 02:01:30.880
this is another 3.5% increase. So that’s quite&nbsp;
significant. And the positive is that it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:30.880 --> 02:01:37.520
been done with a real shift in culture. And,&nbsp;
you know, we want this to be a sustainable shift&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:37.520 --> 02:01:42.800
and a shift where not only do the numbers&nbsp;
change, but the culture changes as well. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:42.800 --> 02:01:47.760
that’s what we are trying to do by intervening&nbsp;
at all, you know, in all different levels.&nbsp;

02:01:48.320 --> 02:01:53.040
So in a year, this is, this&nbsp;
is quite a significant shift.&nbsp;

02:01:54.160 --> 02:01:58.720
Switching to the RES report, which&nbsp;
is the last five years we published,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:58.720 --> 02:02:04.160
you know, the RES 2020 was actually looking&nbsp;
over the five year period, what we see is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:04.160 --> 02:02:08.160
that there are a couple of indicators out of&nbsp;
the nine indicators where we’ve made progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:08.160 --> 02:02:13.760
as the entire NHS and board membership is one&nbsp;
of them. So it’s at a high of 10% right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:14.480 --> 02:02:20.960
And also the difference in disciplinary&nbsp;
proceedings across non BAME colleagues and BAME&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:20.960 --> 02:02:26.080
colleagues. So that gap where you were much more&nbsp;
likely to be put through disciplinary proceedings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:26.080 --> 02:02:31.520
if you are from a BAME background as compared to&nbsp;
white colleagues, that gap has been narrowing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:31.520 --> 02:02:37.920
And in 51% of organisations in the NHS, that gap&nbsp;
is now at zero, which is really, really positive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:37.920 --> 02:02:43.360
But still, I mean, it should be zero everywhere.&nbsp;
But, you know, progress on those two indicators.&nbsp;

02:02:43.360 --> 02:02:47.440
In the other indicators, there hasn’t been&nbsp;
that much progress. And so what we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:47.440 --> 02:02:54.640
doing right now is undertaking, you know, a best&nbsp;
practice review from those trusts which have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:55.520 --> 02:03:00.080
so no trust has really positive results on&nbsp;
all nine indicators of the RES, but there are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:00.080 --> 02:03:04.160
you know, there are bright spots in&nbsp;
different indicators. And we are working&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:04.160 --> 02:03:10.560
with those trusts to identify what we should do.
The first step is, which is in the planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:10.560 --> 02:03:15.280
guidance is action on overhauling recruitment&nbsp;
practices. And that’s been the NHSEI focus as&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:15.280 --> 02:03:20.560
well, because it’s, you know, somebody,&nbsp;
somebody shared this analogy with me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:20.560 --> 02:03:26.000
which I just found really powerful, which&nbsp;
is that there are fences, you know, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:26.000 --> 02:03:31.280
people have to leap over them. We could give&nbsp;
people higher ladders to leap over the fences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:31.280 --> 02:03:36.560
or we can take the fence away. And so overhauling&nbsp;
recruitment practices is very much about&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:36.560 --> 02:03:41.840
taking the fence away and having a level playing&nbsp;
field, than saying please try to climb higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:41.840 --> 02:03:46.160
and higher fences and here are more ladders.&nbsp;
So that is already in the planning guidance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:46.160 --> 02:03:50.400
and the RES is going to focus very much on&nbsp;
overhauling recruitment practices for this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:50.400 --> 02:03:55.200
but a five year strategy is following. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Prerana. I wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:55.200 --> 02:04:00.880
whether Ruth, you had any comment to make on this?
&gt;&gt;Ruth May:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:00.880 --> 02:04:06.400
Thank you. And well, nursing has always been&nbsp;
a very diverse workforce. And I’m delighted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:07.120 --> 02:04:15.920
be supporting Prerana on this not just within NHS&nbsp;
England and Improvement but more widely. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:15.920 --> 02:04:22.320
know that we’ve still got much more to do. There&nbsp;
are some inspiring groups, though. I’ve got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:22.320 --> 02:04:29.040
Black Asian and minority ethnic group of male&nbsp;
nursing leaders, and they’re just so inspiring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:29.040 --> 02:04:36.080
And when I met them this last week, 30% of them&nbsp;
had got promotions in the last year. So there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:36.080 --> 02:04:40.480
some really inspiring stories out there.
I think it’s as Prerana has just said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:40.480 --> 02:04:47.200
how do we make sure that we get that across&nbsp;
all of our organisations and all of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:48.400 --> 02:04:52.640
disciplines of our professions. Happy&nbsp;
to support Prerana’s work completely.&nbsp;

02:04:52.640 --> 02:04:56.240
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Ruth.&nbsp;
And Prerana, thank you very much. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:56.240 --> 02:04:59.680
a very excellent and inspirational&nbsp;
report, so thank you for that.&nbsp;

02:05:00.800 --> 02:05:07.440
Shall we move on then on to maternity services?&nbsp;
And this has really come about as a result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:08.000 --> 02:05:13.360
Donna Ockenden’s report into Shrewsbury&nbsp;
and Telford? And I think, Ruth, you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:13.360 --> 02:05:18.880
going to introduce it, I think. Is that correct?
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Yes, thank you. So there’s a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:18.880 --> 02:05:24.400
elements we want to cover here. The first of which&nbsp;
is Donna Ockenden’s report, which I’m going to ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:24.960 --> 02:05:31.840
Aidan to talk about, and then also the wider&nbsp;
implications, setting that in the safety agenda.&nbsp;

02:05:32.480 --> 02:05:38.800
Second, of course, we’ll ask Sarah-Jane Marsh&nbsp;
as the chair of the maternity transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:38.800 --> 02:05:45.520
board to describe what’s been happening,&nbsp;
and then I’ll conclude on what actions we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:45.520 --> 02:05:52.000
specifically have done in doing in response to&nbsp;
Donna Ockenden’s report. And then I’ll hand over&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:52.000 --> 02:05:58.800
to Simon Eccles to talk about digital.&nbsp;
So if I can ask Aidan to start please.&nbsp;

02:05:58.800 --> 02:06:02.240
&gt;&gt;Aidan Fowler: Thank you, Ruth, and&nbsp;
thank you chair. I’m just going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:03.360 --> 02:06:09.040
blend the two elements of the two papers together,&nbsp;
but first talk about the Ockenden report.&nbsp;

02:06:09.600 --> 02:06:14.640
I think it’s important to start by thanking&nbsp;
Donna Ockenden for her work on this report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:14.640 --> 02:06:20.480
but also and incredibly importantly to thank&nbsp;
the families who have brought this concern&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:20.480 --> 02:06:26.080
to our attention. Many of them have suffered&nbsp;
loss in very difficult circumstances. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:26.080 --> 02:06:31.600
a very challenging thing for them to do. And I&nbsp;
hope what they’ll see today is how seriously we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:31.600 --> 02:06:39.680
take these issues and how huge the response is&nbsp;
to this and has been over the last few years. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:39.680 --> 02:06:46.640
certainly haven’t waited for this report to take&nbsp;
action on maternity, but it’s supplemented it.&nbsp;

02:06:47.200 --> 02:06:51.760
The report was commissioned by the previous&nbsp;
Secretary of State Jeremy Hunt in 2017, initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:51.760 --> 02:06:56.800
into concerns raised about 23 families in&nbsp;
Shrewsbury and Telford Hospital in maternity care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:57.360 --> 02:07:03.760
but subsequently has expanded very significantly,&nbsp;
both as a result of more families coming forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:03.760 --> 02:07:08.480
but also the trust’s own review&nbsp;
of its notes into maternity.&nbsp;

02:07:08.480 --> 02:07:16.400
And the number now stands at 1,862 cases that&nbsp;
are to be reviewed by Donna Ockenden and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:16.400 --> 02:07:23.200
very clinically based team. That is a huge piece&nbsp;
of work, and it was important that we understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:23.200 --> 02:07:28.640
early on any issues that we could act on. And so&nbsp;
a decision was made to write an interim report&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:28.640 --> 02:07:35.200
that was published in December, you’ll see I&nbsp;
think it’s blurred, but you can see this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:35.200 --> 02:07:42.720
relatively thin but very clinical and pragmatic&nbsp;
report on the first 250 of those cases, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:42.720 --> 02:07:49.360
includes the 23 original families, and essentially&nbsp;
a spread across the time period involved.&nbsp;

02:07:49.360 --> 02:07:54.480
Most of the cases are in the 10 years to 2019,&nbsp;
bit there are some more historical cases.&nbsp;

02:07:55.200 --> 02:08:00.400
That report made a number of recommendations,&nbsp;
seven of which were immediate and essential&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:00.400 --> 02:08:06.880
recommendations for the national maternity&nbsp;
system. We have been taking steps to implement&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:06.880 --> 02:08:12.400
all of those and have accepted all of them at face&nbsp;
value and supported them and worked closely with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:12.400 --> 02:08:18.320
Donna on making sure we understand exactly&nbsp;
the aim of them and the importance of them.&nbsp;

02:08:18.320 --> 02:08:24.160
In addition, there were 27 significantly&nbsp;
overlapping recommendations made to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:24.160 --> 02:08:28.400
trust itself, all of which they’ve&nbsp;
accepted and are implemented.&nbsp;

02:08:28.400 --> 02:08:33.680
The trust is already receiving support as a&nbsp;
whole from University Hospitals Birmingham&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:33.680 --> 02:08:40.480
at the instigation of NHSE&amp;I, but in addition, on&nbsp;
maternity has formed a partnership with Sherwood&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:40.480 --> 02:08:47.920
Forest trust, who have experience of making&nbsp;
strides in improving their own maternity services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:47.920 --> 02:08:51.520
and I think there is a lot of overlap&nbsp;
in those experiences so I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:51.520 --> 02:08:57.680
that’s a useful adjunct for it.
And there will be a final report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:57.680 --> 02:09:04.400
which we expect later on this year, which will&nbsp;
give much more detail about those 1,862 cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:05.040 --> 02:09:10.320
We don’t however, in discussion with&nbsp;
Donna Ockenden expect a whole raft of new&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:10.320 --> 02:09:16.240
recommendations, and so we wanted at this point&nbsp;
to get on and implement the recommendations made.&nbsp;

02:09:16.800 --> 02:09:21.600
It is important to note in this context&nbsp;
that there have been very significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:21.600 --> 02:09:28.800
improvements in measures in maternity, measures of&nbsp;
reductions in stillbirths over the last 10 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:28.800 --> 02:09:36.480
and in neonatal and maternal mortality, as well as&nbsp;
small reductions in brain injury. Those data tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:36.480 --> 02:09:42.480
to lag and we are expecting more reductions in&nbsp;
the future, and we are doing more work about that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:42.480 --> 02:09:47.760
But it is notable that there is more to do, and&nbsp;
particularly around equality in these outcomes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:47.760 --> 02:09:52.640
So there’s been a lot of discussions on this&nbsp;
board meeting about inequalities in health care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:53.280 --> 02:09:59.520
no less in maternity, and it is a stark figure&nbsp;
that black mothers have five times the chance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:59.520 --> 02:10:06.480
maternal mortality to white mothers, and&nbsp;
we need to understand and work more on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:06.480 --> 02:10:14.240
these disparities and are doing so.
I just also wanted to talk about the context of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:14.240 --> 02:10:20.240
HSIB, which was one of the asks of where does&nbsp;
HSIB, the healthcare safety investigation branch&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:20.240 --> 02:10:26.640
fit into this agenda? And how does that&nbsp;
fit to the wider patient safety landscape?&nbsp;

02:10:26.640 --> 02:10:31.760
So HSIB were not around at the time&nbsp;
that this report was commissioned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:32.480 --> 02:10:37.440
So the first thing to point out is HSIB could&nbsp;
not have spotted these problems in maternity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:37.440 --> 02:10:42.080
but they were not doing their work at that&nbsp;
time. Initially, HSIB was formed to look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:43.360 --> 02:10:48.480
in depth investigations, 30&nbsp;
reports a year was the expectation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:48.480 --> 02:10:53.280
into safety issues across the NHS.
Later on, the previous Secretary of State&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:53.280 --> 02:10:58.640
added maternity investigations and the expectation&nbsp;
is they would do up to 1,000 investigations a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:00.160 --> 02:11:05.120
based on the recognition that particularly in the&nbsp;
case of Shrewsbury and Telford, the investigations&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:05.120 --> 02:11:09.840
carried out for these cases were of poor quality&nbsp;
and weren’t acted on in a timely fashion.&nbsp;

02:11:10.960 --> 02:11:17.360
Now, there are less than 1,000 cases that&nbsp;
would fit the criteria for HSIB’s maternity&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:17.360 --> 02:11:23.840
investigations, there were probably about 900 a&nbsp;
year that fit and they have been, I think, largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:25.040 --> 02:11:29.920
very effective in doing very in-depth&nbsp;
investigations of these cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:29.920 --> 02:11:36.560
and have written reports on their conclusions, and&nbsp;
have also importantly identified concerns in other&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:36.560 --> 02:11:40.960
areas that we’ve been able to act on much faster.
So I would say that our response in the case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:40.960 --> 02:11:46.160
East Kent, where there is an independent review&nbsp;
being carried out by Bill Kirkup currently, has&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:46.160 --> 02:11:52.240
been much faster and has not required families to&nbsp;
have to fight and campaign for some time for us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:52.240 --> 02:11:57.600
look at those concerns. And they are notifying us&nbsp;
of challenges where we see them, and we are acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:57.600 --> 02:12:03.200
on those notably with the maternity services&nbsp;
support programme that’s been so important in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:03.200 --> 02:12:08.560
supporting trusts that see challenging maternity.
So HSIB are part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:09.920 --> 02:12:17.120
our approach to assurance in the system, they&nbsp;
are able to do investigations in depth, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:17.120 --> 02:12:22.800
they will be part of whatever happens next.
So we have in NHSI responsibility for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:22.800 --> 02:12:26.640
governance around HR, finance, etc&nbsp;
for HSIB, but the investigations sit&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:26.640 --> 02:12:32.320
under the purview of the department of health&nbsp;
and social care. It is their decision as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:32.320 --> 02:12:38.080
what happens with maternity investigations. But&nbsp;
I think many of us would like to see training&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:38.080 --> 02:12:46.480
and support around reintroducing those into the&nbsp;
system. And over time, my expectation is that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:46.480 --> 02:12:51.760
likely to be what happens and HSIB will be&nbsp;
part of the support and training of staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:51.760 --> 02:12:58.240
to carry out those investigations&nbsp;
alongside our own improved serious incident&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:59.120 --> 02:13:04.880
investigation process, referred to at the moment&nbsp;
as PSIRF, the patient safety investigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:05.840 --> 02:13:11.040
system rather than the current safety&nbsp;
investigation, serious incident framework rather.&nbsp;

02:13:21.280 --> 02:13:22.480
Can you hear me?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:23.120 --> 02:13:30.000
you went mute for a minute.&nbsp;
Yeah. You’ve gone mute again.&nbsp;

02:13:30.000 --> 02:13:34.320
&gt;&gt;Aidan Fowler: My notes are pressing&nbsp;
on the spacebar. Apologies for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:35.840 --> 02:13:44.240
So I also have laid out in the paper how this fits&nbsp;
with the wider patient safety system. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:44.240 --> 02:13:50.320
from the point of view of focusing on maternity,&nbsp;
we are seeing some important changes. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:50.320 --> 02:13:54.320
would expect Sarah-Jane will want to talk&nbsp;
more about the maternal transformation plan.&nbsp;

02:13:54.320 --> 02:14:00.640
In the current maternity transformation plan,&nbsp;
patient safety sits as workstream two of 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:01.200 --> 02:14:09.520
with specific focuses in this arena.
What we’re looking at is a new approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:10.240 --> 02:14:15.120
for the maternal transformation programme as&nbsp;
it reaches five years to continue the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:15.120 --> 02:14:21.440
in which we’ll see, one, the design of the patient&nbsp;
safety strategy reflected divided into insight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:21.440 --> 02:14:26.320
knowing what’s happening in the system,&nbsp;
which would include the HSIB reports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:27.040 --> 02:14:31.600
also the infrastructure, that middle section&nbsp;
and you will hear more from Ruth about what that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:31.600 --> 02:14:37.280
means for staffing, equipment and so on and where&nbsp;
investment is going into that very significantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:37.280 --> 02:14:44.080
as well as the programmes of improvement laid&nbsp;
out in relation to antenatal care, intrapartum&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:44.080 --> 02:14:50.320
care and postpartum care in a simplified system&nbsp;
that we hope will be much clearer for people and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:50.320 --> 02:14:56.080
particularly for hard working staff to get their&nbsp;
head around and engage with, as well as other&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:56.080 --> 02:15:04.080
elements we are obviously clearly supporting very&nbsp;
strongly on the work to spot deterioration and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:04.080 --> 02:15:11.520
manage that more effectively on culture.
And also, we are trying to simplify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:11.520 --> 02:15:17.680
way in the insights section reporting&nbsp;
occurs. For example, up until recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:17.680 --> 02:15:22.720
maternal deaths would be reported in about&nbsp;
seven different ways to different systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:22.720 --> 02:15:27.440
which is hugely burdensome for staff to generate&nbsp;
the same information. So we’re simplifying that.&nbsp;

02:15:27.440 --> 02:15:33.520
So there’s a lot of work going on to align&nbsp;
the approach taken in maternity to our wider&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:33.520 --> 02:15:39.920
patient safety goals, to simplify it,&nbsp;
and also to remove some of the silos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:39.920 --> 02:15:44.880
so patient safety will be a theme across the&nbsp;
whole new maternity transformation programme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:44.880 --> 02:15:50.080
rather than a single item within it. At that&nbsp;
point, I’m going to hand back to Ruth or on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:50.080 --> 02:15:54.400
to Sarah-Jane for further comment.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you. Sarah-Jane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:54.400 --> 02:15:56.720
if I can ask you.
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:56.720 --> 02:16:00.800
Thanks, Ruth. And good afternoon, everybody.&nbsp;
I’m Sarah Jane Marsh and I chair the NHS England&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:00.800 --> 02:16:05.280
Improvement maternity transformation programme.
Indeed I have done that for the last five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:05.280 --> 02:16:11.040
and next Tuesday, we’ll be celebrating five&nbsp;
years of the programme and five years since&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:11.040 --> 02:16:15.520
the publication of Better Births. So I just&nbsp;
wanted to pause for a moment before I go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:15.520 --> 02:16:21.200
to the things where we know we need to do better,&nbsp;
to focus on the things that we have done really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:21.200 --> 02:16:25.200
really well and that we feel proud of. And I felt&nbsp;
I need to do that on behalf of the thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:25.200 --> 02:16:30.560
staff that work in maternity services across the&nbsp;
country that are very proud of the achievements.&nbsp;

02:16:30.560 --> 02:16:36.000
So as Aidan said, we’ve seen a 25%&nbsp;
reduction in stillbirths. We’ve seen a 23%&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:36.000 --> 02:16:42.560
reduction in neonatal mortality for babies born&nbsp;
over 24 weeks gestation. We’ve seen a 9% reduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:43.120 --> 02:16:48.800
in maternal mortality. We’ve seen reductions&nbsp;
in babies born with brain injuries, we’ve seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:48.800 --> 02:16:54.720
preterm birth falling, and we’ve seen women’s&nbsp;
overall satisfaction with maternity services&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:54.720 --> 02:17:01.040
never higher. And indeed, between the last two&nbsp;
CQC surveys in maternity, we saw the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:01.040 --> 02:17:07.519
set of improvements of any clinical service in&nbsp;
the NHS as measured through the CQC surveys.&nbsp;

02:17:08.240 --> 02:17:13.359
So we’ve been doing a tremendous job over the&nbsp;
last five years. But we are the first group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:13.359 --> 02:17:18.880
people to say that there are also things&nbsp;
where we know we’ve needed to do better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:19.439 --> 02:17:25.439
And in particular, throughout the whole five&nbsp;
years, it’s been laced with really some awful&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:25.439 --> 02:17:33.280
stories, as Aidan has described, where we know&nbsp;
that in either individual pockets in services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:33.280 --> 02:17:38.880
or sometimes across entire services, we have&nbsp;
let women and families down collectively.&nbsp;

02:17:39.439 --> 02:17:46.639
And often we do see clustering. We can’t say that&nbsp;
they are related to just one-off incidents or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:47.520 --> 02:17:53.281
isolated happenings. And so what we’ve done over&nbsp;
the past year or so really sort of building on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:53.840 --> 02:17:58.240
some of the early findings from places, not just&nbsp;
Donna Ockenden’s review, but learning from places&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:58.240 --> 02:18:07.280
like East Kent too where we know we’ve also had&nbsp;
issues, is to try to understand the root causes&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:07.280 --> 02:18:12.880
of why some of these things happen, and why some&nbsp;
of the things that we’ve tried to do nationally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:12.880 --> 02:18:17.520
despite all of our best efforts, don’t&nbsp;
seem to have been able to have been applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:17.520 --> 02:18:22.081
universally. They’ve worked in some places, but&nbsp;
somehow they’ve managed to pass some individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:22.720 --> 02:18:29.520
units and services by. And we’ve come down to&nbsp;
three main root causes. The first is where we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:29.520 --> 02:18:36.160
got a poor leadership culture, and therefore&nbsp;
a poor culture in the service, everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:36.160 --> 02:18:42.479
else becomes more difficult. Behaviours become&nbsp;
difficult, transparency becomes more difficult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:42.479 --> 02:18:48.000
the units are less likely to implement the changes&nbsp;
that we lead through maternity transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:48.000 --> 02:18:55.040
for example, the saving babies’ lives care bundle.
The second is that many of our units where we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:55.040 --> 02:19:01.439
had difficulties have struggled with two things in&nbsp;
particular. One, the overall levels of staff they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:01.439 --> 02:19:07.759
have available to deploy right across maternity&nbsp;
pathways, and particularly on delivery suites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:07.760 --> 02:19:12.000
where we know we need to get staffing numbers&nbsp;
right for the numbers of women that are coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:12.000 --> 02:19:17.680
through the services, but also some of our digital&nbsp;
systems and how they make on occasion things more&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:17.680 --> 02:19:23.201
difficult for our teams rather than facilitating&nbsp;
our teams to be able to provide a great service.&nbsp;

02:19:23.840 --> 02:19:31.760
And the third are failures of board governance,&nbsp;
and troubles not just at board level, but then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:31.760 --> 02:19:37.920
up through the system in terms of genuine insights&nbsp;
into what is happening in services. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:37.920 --> 02:19:42.960
there isn’t one or two things that you can look at&nbsp;
in maternity to know if there’s an issue or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:42.960 --> 02:19:47.120
If there were four or five data points,&nbsp;
you could look at those and you’d know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:47.120 --> 02:19:50.240
then we would have cracked this a long&nbsp;
time ago. It just isn’t like that.&nbsp;

02:19:50.800 --> 02:19:56.080
And so our big focus going forwards is,&nbsp;
one, to have that focus on training and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:56.080 --> 02:20:01.840
that training to be really around the team,&nbsp;
with no point training people in isolation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:01.840 --> 02:20:06.960
because in the small hours of the morning when&nbsp;
we know sometimes we get into difficulties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:06.960 --> 02:20:10.960
it’s the way that people work and respond&nbsp;
together. So having the proper dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:10.960 --> 02:20:15.760
time for training and leadership training.
The second is the work that we’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:15.760 --> 02:20:19.520
doing around staffing and digital&nbsp;
systems and Ruth and Simon Eccles&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:19.520 --> 02:20:23.840
are going to talk around those in a moment.
Then the third is the launch of the board&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:23.840 --> 02:20:29.520
development programme, which we actually did&nbsp;
yesterday, to get particularly our non-executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:29.520 --> 02:20:33.600
director champions, but also our executive&nbsp;
board safety in maternity champions as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:34.160 --> 02:20:39.200
to really, really understand the questions&nbsp;
they need to be asking, the way they need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:39.200 --> 02:20:43.840
be engaging inside the maternity services&nbsp;
and across the system to get this right.&nbsp;

02:20:44.399 --> 02:20:48.719
So we think we’ve got some major successes that&nbsp;
we can build on in the maternity transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:48.720 --> 02:20:53.920
programme. What we’ve now got to do is to make&nbsp;
sure that we can get into every single unit&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:54.479 --> 02:21:00.479
in England, and to make sure that the things&nbsp;
that we know work are universally applied so&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:00.479 --> 02:21:07.200
that no woman or family ever gets behind, is ever&nbsp;
left behind by anything that we do in maternity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:08.080 --> 02:21:11.840
Thank you. I’m going to hand over Ruth and&nbsp;
she’s going to pick up a couple of those themes.&nbsp;

02:21:12.479 --> 02:21:15.359
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Sarah-Jane, thanks very much for&nbsp;
that. I should have introduced you properly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:15.359 --> 02:21:21.120
beginning actually. But Sarah-Jane, well, it&nbsp;
says in the paper, but she runs the Women and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:21.120 --> 02:21:26.319
Children’s Hospital in Birmingham and has done a&nbsp;
phenomenal job as well helping out on the testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:26.319 --> 02:21:30.559
programme as well recently. So Sarah-Jane, it is&nbsp;
really good to see you. Sorry, Ruth, over to you.&nbsp;

02:21:30.560 --> 02:21:34.479
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you. Thank you very much.&nbsp;
And thank you to Aidan and Sarah Jane.&nbsp;

02:21:34.479 --> 02:21:40.879
So let me just first acknowledge Donna&nbsp;
Ockenden’s first report into the maternity&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:40.880 --> 02:21:47.760
services at Shrewsbury and Telford trust.&nbsp;
It’s humbling, it’s harrowing findings&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:47.760 --> 02:21:54.880
of the tragic experiences that people received&nbsp;
at Shrewsbury and Telford maternity centres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:55.840 --> 02:22:02.399
and I recognise the deep and lasting impact&nbsp;
on those families who have lost loved ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:02.399 --> 02:22:07.839
and those who continue to live with the&nbsp;
injury and the trauma that has been caused.&nbsp;

02:22:08.640 --> 02:22:14.880
So Donna Ockenden set out in her report seven&nbsp;
immediate and essential actions for maternity&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:14.880 --> 02:22:20.480
services across England. This report was&nbsp;
published on Thursday, the 10th of December,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:21.520 --> 02:22:29.280
and Amanda, myself and Steve wrote out to the&nbsp;
service on Monday the 14th of December, asking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:29.280 --> 02:22:38.319
trusts to proceed to implement the full set of the&nbsp;
seven Ockenden immediate and essential actions.&nbsp;

02:22:39.200 --> 02:22:47.040
But we identified 12 urgent clinical priorities,&nbsp;
and we did that collectively, myself, Aidan, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:47.040 --> 02:22:53.600
Donna Ockenden herself. We asked trusts to confirm&nbsp;
their compliance with these immediate actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:54.240 --> 02:23:00.559
to review the report at their next public board&nbsp;
meeting, to complete a board assurance tool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:00.560 --> 02:23:07.040
which included assessment against the NICE&nbsp;
guidance relating to maternity, compliance against&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:07.040 --> 02:23:16.160
the CNST safety actions, and a current workforce&nbsp;
gap analysis. We received 100% returns from all&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:16.160 --> 02:23:22.240
maternity services on the 15th of February&nbsp;
and we’re now reviewing those submissions.&nbsp;

02:23:24.000 --> 02:23:30.160
As Sarah-Jane and Aidan have already said, the&nbsp;
themes and the reviews have said we need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:30.160 --> 02:23:36.880
look at workforce numbers, culture and leadership&nbsp;
and strengthening the surveillance of our trusts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:38.479 --> 02:23:44.319
And so with this in mind, I’m delighted that the&nbsp;
board today have agreed in the planning guidance&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:44.960 --> 02:23:54.880
to invest an additional 95.9 million pounds in&nbsp;
21/22 to support the system to address all seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:54.880 --> 02:24:00.319
of those immediate and&nbsp;
essential actions consistently.&nbsp;

02:24:00.319 --> 02:24:06.000
I’m very pleased that 83.8 million of&nbsp;
that will go directly to the system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:06.640 --> 02:24:14.640
So it’s allocated for the greatest impact&nbsp;
on safety. So 46.7 million will go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:14.640 --> 02:24:22.080
midwifery workforce. And we will ask all maternity&nbsp;
services across England to ensure that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:22.080 --> 02:24:31.280
are meeting the birth rate plus requirements and&nbsp;
that’s a midwifery workforce assessment tool. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:31.280 --> 02:24:39.280
will I’m sure give us 1,000 more midwifery posts.
The second big tranche of money will go to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:39.280 --> 02:24:45.679
obstetric workforce, 10.6 million. And there are&nbsp;
some actions within the report that have a direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:45.680 --> 02:24:52.560
impact on obstetric capacity, foetal wellbeing&nbsp;
lead and the consultant ward rounds. We are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:52.560 --> 02:25:00.000
therefore funding an increase in the consultant&nbsp;
time, equal to 80 whole time equivalents in 21/22.&nbsp;

02:25:00.960 --> 02:25:05.920
And as we’ve already heard about,&nbsp;
multidisciplinary training is so important. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:05.920 --> 02:25:13.120
we’re funding 26.5 million pounds in the capacity&nbsp;
to release clinicians for training because I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:13.120 --> 02:25:19.599
that has been challenging for organisations to do.
We know by investing in workforce capacity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:19.600 --> 02:25:25.359
allow teams to train together will improve&nbsp;
the way multidisciplinary teams work&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:25.359 --> 02:25:32.399
together. The remainder of the funding will&nbsp;
be used to support some national and regional&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:32.960 --> 02:25:39.920
infrastructure including appoint two whole time&nbsp;
equivalent consultant obstetricians in each region&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:39.920 --> 02:25:48.880
and seven deputy regional chief midwives.
It will also be include 3 million pounds for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:48.880 --> 02:25:57.359
an independent senior advocate as Donna Ockenden&nbsp;
has requested, and this will ensure consistency&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:57.359 --> 02:26:02.479
across England. And that role will be developed&nbsp;
and of course, therefore funded nationally.&nbsp;

02:26:03.600 --> 02:26:08.000
And finally, we know that&nbsp;
we’re going to need to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:08.000 --> 02:26:11.439
like we have with the international&nbsp;
nurse recruitment, a whole programme&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:11.439 --> 02:26:16.799
around international recruitment of midwives,&nbsp;
and 5 million pounds will go towards that.&nbsp;

02:26:19.040 --> 02:26:26.000
The important bit for me around this then&nbsp;
is about how we work to make that happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:26.000 --> 02:26:32.240
how we now go and implement this. So we&nbsp;
will conclude by working with partners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:32.800 --> 02:26:38.080
Health Education England, of course, Wendy&nbsp;
Reid and Mark Radford in making sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:38.080 --> 02:26:43.120
we are able to have that sustainable&nbsp;
supply of obstetricians and midwives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:43.680 --> 02:26:47.840
Working with our LMS partners with the Royal&nbsp;
College of Obstetricians and Gynaecology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:48.640 --> 02:26:55.840
and of course with the Royal College of Midwives.
So we will work together to ensure that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:55.840 --> 02:27:02.000
implement these national recommendations&nbsp;
so that we do have the safest&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:02.000 --> 02:27:09.040
care in our maternity care in England.
That’s all I have to say at the moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:09.040 --> 02:27:14.800
chair. I’m happy to take questions or if we want&nbsp;
to just have a tiny bit on digital from Simon&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:15.680 --> 02:27:19.520
first, then I’m happy with that too.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Shall we just take a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:20.240 --> 02:27:24.399
amount of time with Simon on digital&nbsp;
then come back to more broader questions?&nbsp;

02:27:24.399 --> 02:27:28.639
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Simon Eccles:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:28.640 --> 02:27:36.640
Thank you. Thank you very much, David. The&nbsp;
maternity digital transformation programme&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:36.640 --> 02:27:41.920
aims to give every woman access to their own&nbsp;
digital maternity record across the whole of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:41.920 --> 02:27:47.280
their pathway of care, and to give them control&nbsp;
and understanding of the whole of that pathway.&nbsp;

02:27:49.680 --> 02:27:55.439
We are producing national standards and&nbsp;
ensuring adherence to the maternity care&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:55.439 --> 02:27:59.839
data set in order to give the data&nbsp;
that Ruth has just outlined we need.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:00.399 --> 02:28:04.399
We’re supporting the business&nbsp;
change to ensure that the whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:04.399 --> 02:28:10.000
every partner in that pathway of care is&nbsp;
accessing the same information, is providing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:10.000 --> 02:28:14.880
to the woman, and ensuring that each part&nbsp;
of the pathway can see the same information.&nbsp;

02:28:15.840 --> 02:28:21.680
We’ve been running 20 small-scale pilots of this&nbsp;
programme of work, and are now moving to four&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:22.319 --> 02:28:29.120
much larger regional models involving millions&nbsp;
of patients. The aim being to get to 100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:30.479 --> 02:28:36.799
take-up of digital records across the whole&nbsp;
pathway of care, ideally by 2024, or we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:36.800 --> 02:28:41.360
looking at all possible ways of accelerating&nbsp;
the rollout and benefits of the programme.&nbsp;

02:28:43.200 --> 02:28:49.200
We’re looking to produce a market of products that&nbsp;
are truly useful to the women they serve and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:49.200 --> 02:28:53.840
improve the information available publicly&nbsp;
to women on the NHS website. To that end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:53.840 --> 02:28:58.560
we developed the I decide product to help with&nbsp;
the specific recommendation from Donna Ockenden’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:58.560 --> 02:29:04.960
report to produce much better information.
And lastly, we’re aiming to ensure that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:04.960 --> 02:29:11.359
maternity record forms part of the full life&nbsp;
course record and is not seen as an isolated&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:11.359 --> 02:29:18.319
segment, but fits into a full life course record.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:18.319 --> 02:29:25.200
Thank you very much, Simon. So we’ve heard from&nbsp;
Sarah-Jane, Aidan, Simon, and of course, Ruth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:25.760 --> 02:29:31.680
Have the board got any questions they’d like&nbsp;
to put to them? I know, I mean, David Roberts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:31.680 --> 02:29:36.560
you’ve taken a particular interest&nbsp;
in this. Would you like to kick off?&nbsp;

02:29:36.560 --> 02:29:41.840
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Yeah, thank you.&nbsp;
Thank you, David. And, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:41.840 --> 02:29:49.439
it is one of the most sort of heartrending and&nbsp;
hardest things that we face into I think. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:49.439 --> 02:29:54.799
thing that I’m curious about, and Sarah-Jane,&nbsp;
and thank you for the work you’ve done, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:55.760 --> 02:30:03.040
is this issue of leadership and culture, which&nbsp;
seems as we go back over some of the incidents and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:03.680 --> 02:30:12.880
the tragedies that we’ve seen over a considerable&nbsp;
period of time, to be a sort of a common cause.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:14.479 --> 02:30:22.879
And therefore, I’m interested as to how we’re&nbsp;
going to tackle that. Because ultimately, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:22.880 --> 02:30:28.479
is also about sort of, it’s not just tone from the&nbsp;
top, it’s actions from the top, and therefore it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:28.479 --> 02:30:36.559
about, have we got the right people in the right&nbsp;
place? Are we confident in the ways of working?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:37.840 --> 02:30:45.600
And linked to that, have we got the right data&nbsp;
etc? But I’m curious to hear your thoughts on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:45.600 --> 02:30:51.920
how we’re going to try and address the leadership&nbsp;
and culture issue because it strikes me as without&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:51.920 --> 02:30:59.359
addressing that the rest becomes quite hard.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:59.359 --> 02:31:01.439
Ruth, do you want to?
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:01.439 --> 02:31:03.040
question first was Sarah-Jane.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:04.160 --> 02:31:08.639
Either would be great or both would be fabulous.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: You go first, Sarah-Jane.&nbsp;

02:31:08.640 --> 02:31:12.319
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: Thanks for the question,&nbsp;
David. It’s something that we spend a lot of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:12.319 --> 02:31:18.559
debating. I think one of the issues that we&nbsp;
can see in the services where things go wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:18.560 --> 02:31:23.520
is that they seem to be operating outside&nbsp;
of the line of sight of the board.&nbsp;

02:31:25.200 --> 02:31:29.359
Often people in the past, I do think this&nbsp;
has changed quite dramatically recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:29.359 --> 02:31:35.920
but often in the past maternity services were just&nbsp;
sort of left to get on. And often there wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:35.920 --> 02:31:42.319
particularly visible data and there just wasn’t&nbsp;
a general awareness of what good looked like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:42.319 --> 02:31:46.080
We weren’t always listening to women, I think&nbsp;
too, on some boards as we should have done.&nbsp;

02:31:46.640 --> 02:31:51.680
So part of it has been to open that out by having&nbsp;
our board level maternity safety champions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:51.680 --> 02:31:57.280
and we’ve had those on the executive side now&nbsp;
for several years. And I think that has started&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:57.280 --> 02:32:01.359
to make an impact. We have board, you know, teams&nbsp;
going to the board so people can make their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:01.359 --> 02:32:06.639
assessments about whether they think that progress&nbsp;
has been made, and all of those kinds of things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:06.640 --> 02:32:11.120
But we’ve also got some really specific&nbsp;
culture tools that we’ve been using. We did&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:11.120 --> 02:32:16.399
a culture survey in every single maternity unit in&nbsp;
the country as part of our maternity and neonatal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:17.120 --> 02:32:21.280
service improvement programme.&nbsp;
That highlighted some themes, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:21.280 --> 02:32:28.880
the team worked with every unit in the country&nbsp;
to come up with a personal plan to that unit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:28.880 --> 02:32:34.640
to see what could be done to improve things.
But we do come back to this recurrent theme&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:34.640 --> 02:32:38.320
of people having time to lead&nbsp;
and time to do the development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:39.439 --> 02:32:44.240
And that’s again why that’s an area&nbsp;
that’s really been prioritised of late. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:44.800 --> 02:32:49.840
there’s many, many different things that&nbsp;
we’re doing. But we can see the tide turning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:49.840 --> 02:32:54.160
as I said in my introduction, it really is&nbsp;
about making sure that we can reach out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:54.800 --> 02:33:00.479
everywhere and make sure that that good practice&nbsp;
that has worked in most places reaches everybody.&nbsp;

02:33:00.479 --> 02:33:05.200
And I think the midwifery side is really advanced&nbsp;
with having chief midwives in every region and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:05.760 --> 02:33:09.760
all of those things. That’s one of the&nbsp;
reasons that Ruth just talked about having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:09.760 --> 02:33:14.640
chief obstetrician in every region as well. So&nbsp;
we’ve got people that we can send to go and look&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:14.640 --> 02:33:19.840
and have conversations and see whether they think&nbsp;
there are issues, whether we need to give extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:19.840 --> 02:33:23.120
training or whether we need to change people&nbsp;
around because they’re no longer working. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:24.080 --> 02:33:28.399
lots and lots of different interventions. But&nbsp;
the culture remains our number one thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:28.399 --> 02:33:33.759
we focus on as the transformation programme.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Just to build on that very slightly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:33.760 --> 02:33:38.720
agreeing with Sarah-Jane. So we know&nbsp;
that from all of the research that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:38.720 --> 02:33:41.680
when people learn together,&nbsp;
when people train together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:42.240 --> 02:33:48.399
they’ll work better together long term. And that’s&nbsp;
why a significant amount of this investment is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:48.399 --> 02:33:54.559
make sure that people are released to go and learn&nbsp;
together. And that I’m sure will help the culture.&nbsp;

02:33:55.200 --> 02:33:59.040
Of course, it’s all very well, but&nbsp;
actually, unless you’ve got enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:59.040 --> 02:34:03.120
midwives and obstetricians, it doesn’t&nbsp;
help. And that’s again, why a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:03.120 --> 02:34:07.359
this investment is focused on increasing&nbsp;
the numbers of obstetricians and midwives.&nbsp;

02:34:07.920 --> 02:34:13.520
I guess what Donna Ockenden and we are&nbsp;
asking boards is for boards to focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:13.520 --> 02:34:20.399
on maternity services, to really understand&nbsp;
them, and to support them and to hear the voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:20.399 --> 02:34:29.839
of maternity services. And all of the actions in&nbsp;
the seven actions that Donna outlined are saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:29.840 --> 02:34:35.280
just that and so I am hoping that our boards,&nbsp;
and challenging our boards to do just that.&nbsp;

02:34:35.280 --> 02:34:41.519
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Thank you, Ruth. And another&nbsp;
statistic that is also truly horrifying is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:41.520 --> 02:34:50.720
the relative inequality of outcome, which was&nbsp;
mentioned. And I see Wol’s put his hand up maybe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:51.280 --> 02:34:58.399
you know, but it’s just another shocking&nbsp;
statistic. And again, it would be interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:58.399 --> 02:35:03.200
to hear how we’re seeking to address that.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Wol, did you want to come in?&nbsp;

02:35:03.200 --> 02:35:08.479
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: I mean, I do agree with David’s&nbsp;
point and I’d like to hear the answer to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:08.479 --> 02:35:10.645
before I come in, if that’s all right.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ruth.&nbsp;

02:35:10.646 --> 02:35:13.359
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: I was just going to say,&nbsp;
Sarah-Jane, do you want to do some&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:13.359 --> 02:35:16.799
of the work around continuity of care?
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: I am very happy to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:16.800 --> 02:35:22.720
I think in the latest Embrace report, we have&nbsp;
made a small dent actually. And now I think for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:22.720 --> 02:35:26.720
black women are four times more likely&nbsp;
rather than five times more likely, which is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:26.720 --> 02:35:33.760
you know, not amazing, but is a small, small step.
We’ve done an awful lot of work on this and trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:33.760 --> 02:35:40.160
to really work with our teams to make people&nbsp;
comfortable with delivering uneven care, i.e. more&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:40.160 --> 02:35:44.800
care to some women than others to try and level&nbsp;
up overall outcomes, which isn’t something that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:44.800 --> 02:35:50.080
the natural strengths often of clinical teams.&nbsp;
So we were very particular over the last year&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:50.080 --> 02:35:55.840
about making sure that women from ethnic minority&nbsp;
backgrounds have more face-to-face appointments&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:55.840 --> 02:36:04.080
and had a lower threshold, a lower risk threshold&nbsp;
for which we would call them in to escalate care.&nbsp;

02:36:04.080 --> 02:36:08.080
So we’ve done quite a few immediate things&nbsp;
over the last 12 months that I hope have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:08.080 --> 02:36:14.319
impactful. But longer term, we have focused&nbsp;
our attentions on continuity of care. We’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:14.319 --> 02:36:19.200
talked about this at NHS England certainly board&nbsp;
before, we know the difference that this can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:19.200 --> 02:36:26.479
to outcomes for all women. But what we’ve asked&nbsp;
for is for it to be differentially implemented&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:27.120 --> 02:36:32.240
so that women from minority ethnic backgrounds or&nbsp;
from deprived postcodes, where we also have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:32.240 --> 02:36:40.880
same issues, have greater access to continuity&nbsp;
than white women or those from wealthier areas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:41.439 --> 02:36:46.799
And in the last data that we looked at,&nbsp;
that was working. We were disproportionately&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:47.760 --> 02:36:52.000
giving continuity to women from minority&nbsp;
ethnic backgrounds. And indeed, we’ve got&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:52.000 --> 02:36:56.479
some huge champions of this out in the service.
So it will take a little while to see whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:56.479 --> 02:37:02.719
that feeds through into the outcome data. But&nbsp;
I think as all the teams, we are absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:02.720 --> 02:37:08.479
focused on this, and have used the opportunities,&nbsp;
albeit it has been devastating, because we lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:08.479 --> 02:37:13.040
several women in the first wave, but we’ve&nbsp;
used the opportunity of the pandemic to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:13.040 --> 02:37:16.640
we need to do things very, very differently&nbsp;
now. And I think that has been embraced.&nbsp;

02:37:16.640 --> 02:37:21.680
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: And one final point from me,&nbsp;
and I’ll be quiet, but there are also some quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:21.680 --> 02:37:32.800
impressive digital tools targeting areas, women&nbsp;
from areas of deprivation, women of ethnicity. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:32.800 --> 02:37:39.439
again, they can be a powerful community link to&nbsp;
support women as well as our own efforts. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:39.439 --> 02:37:45.679
think it’s how we link some of those together,&nbsp;
I think is also an important way in which we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:45.680 --> 02:37:52.000
can help improve outcomes for everyone, but&nbsp;
particularly areas where we know we’ve got work&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:52.000 --> 02:37:57.040
to do. Thank you for the work you’ve done. And&nbsp;
thank you for this. And David, I’m sure it’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:57.040 --> 02:38:02.240
subject that we’ll come back to regularly.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: It certainly is. And Wol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:02.240 --> 02:38:04.399
have you still got a question?
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Yeah, that’s a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:05.439 --> 02:38:10.319
segue into what I was going to ask about&nbsp;
that last bit of what David you both said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:10.319 --> 02:38:14.479
which is we can come back to this.
So I mean, you’ve given us all of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:14.479 --> 02:38:20.719
a really good, strong impression of&nbsp;
the work you’re doing and have done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:20.720 --> 02:38:24.480
and that you know, there’s no complacency&nbsp;
at all, which comes through in spades.&nbsp;

02:38:25.040 --> 02:38:29.600
I have to say it’s, it’s, it’s one of&nbsp;
those difficult reads I’ve ever had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:29.600 --> 02:38:34.080
in this sort of environment, it’s just,&nbsp;
you know, I find myself a bit sort of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:36.240 --> 02:38:39.920
well, just completely uncomfortable, let’s&nbsp;
put it that way. Now, the point about this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:39.920 --> 02:38:45.520
that you mentioned culture, and culture is&nbsp;
critical. And it’s a two-way street. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:45.520 --> 02:38:48.880
it’s the other way I’m looking. So one&nbsp;
is the maternity unit. The other is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:48.880 --> 02:38:53.280
board. Sometimes boards, even though you’re&nbsp;
being transparent and give them information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:53.840 --> 02:38:56.720
don’t know what to do with it, don’t know&nbsp;
how to look at it, don’t how to treat it.&nbsp;

02:38:57.600 --> 02:39:01.200
And so, can you just talk a bit about&nbsp;
the support you’re giving to help them&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:01.200 --> 02:39:06.559
foster a culture where people want to report&nbsp;
stuff that doesn’t look very good. And how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:06.560 --> 02:39:11.520
then address what they do about it. Because you&nbsp;
know, the danger is if you get that balance wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:12.080 --> 02:39:16.080
people will quickly shut down and start to&nbsp;
sort of, you know, do all the wrong things.&nbsp;

02:39:16.080 --> 02:39:21.600
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Yeah, thank you, Wol. I’ll start and&nbsp;
then some of my colleagues may wish to come in.&nbsp;

02:39:22.399 --> 02:39:29.439
Absolutely. And we have to support boards&nbsp;
to do this because the maternity services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:30.160 --> 02:39:37.760
they aren’t easy to, to really get underneath.
So one of the things we’ve been doing since 2018&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:37.760 --> 02:39:45.359
is to have a maternity support programme, where&nbsp;
we have been able to support going into boards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:45.359 --> 02:39:50.719
having that conversations with boards directly&nbsp;
before going into the maternity units themselves.&nbsp;

02:39:52.000 --> 02:39:58.240
Since 2020, we’ve strengthened that so that we&nbsp;
are now looking not at supporting those units&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:58.240 --> 02:40:03.599
just when they are flagged with the CQC,&nbsp;
but those are the ones, also ones that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:04.399 --> 02:40:10.319
struggling. And that’s where we’re at now. And&nbsp;
that’s the most important that we’re doing.&nbsp;

02:40:10.319 --> 02:40:15.840
We do know that we’re having to work through&nbsp;
boards, but with our consultant obstetricians&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:15.840 --> 02:40:21.840
and midwifery, and our midwives, and&nbsp;
having our support people going in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:22.800 --> 02:40:28.640
we’ve had some really good feedback from boards.&nbsp;
And it’s been really interesting feedback too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:28.640 --> 02:40:36.080
from the staff, that they’ve seen boards listening&nbsp;
more and understanding more, as after they’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:36.080 --> 02:40:42.399
part of the support programme. I wondered if&nbsp;
any of my other colleagues wish to comment.&nbsp;

02:40:42.399 --> 02:40:49.920
&gt;&gt;Aidan Fowler: So if I can come in there, Ruth. I&nbsp;
think this is really important point. And there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:49.920 --> 02:40:55.200
lots of complexity to it. But one of the things&nbsp;
that is very striking is that there is an element&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:55.200 --> 02:41:00.399
of isolation in some of these units that they&nbsp;
are not sharing practice across them and we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:00.399 --> 02:41:04.960
trying to make sure that that doesn’t occur.&nbsp;
And certainly in Shrewsbury and Telford, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:04.960 --> 02:41:10.319
practice was quite separate, if you like from the&nbsp;
rest of the world, and we often refer to remote&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:10.319 --> 02:41:16.639
and rural, they’re not always remote or rural, but&nbsp;
they are isolated in some sense from the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:16.640 --> 02:41:23.520
the system. Recently, two of the patient safety&nbsp;
team went on an investigation training programme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:23.520 --> 02:41:27.520
these things exist, and this was run by the&nbsp;
charity Baby Lifeline. And there were Shrewsbury&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:27.520 --> 02:41:33.121
and Telford staff on it and they were very, very&nbsp;
struck. The staff became really quite emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:34.000 --> 02:41:40.240
when they realised how wrong they had been doing&nbsp;
the investigations and the impact that it had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:40.800 --> 02:41:44.800
And they had no understanding, they just&nbsp;
simply hadn’t understood how to do it properly.&nbsp;

02:41:44.800 --> 02:41:50.319
So I think you know, there have been huge strides&nbsp;
in maternity, the normal distribution has moved in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:50.319 --> 02:41:54.080
the right direction, but there has been a bit&nbsp;
of a tail. And I think the maternity support&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:54.080 --> 02:42:01.040
programme has been really important in bringing&nbsp;
that tail up to the levels we expect of others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:01.040 --> 02:42:06.640
but also in in challenging this isolation and&nbsp;
looking at things like the maternity learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:06.640 --> 02:42:14.240
support networks, and making sure that there&nbsp;
is a mix of providers in those so that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:14.240 --> 02:42:20.240
are strong providers to support those with&nbsp;
challenges. And you know, that sharing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:20.240 --> 02:42:25.920
collaboration of learning that needs to occur.
&gt;&gt;Sarah-Jane Marsh: David, just one final point&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:25.920 --> 02:42:31.680
to add, from the session that we did yesterday&nbsp;
with all the board safety champions, we had women&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:31.680 --> 02:42:36.880
with us who told really powerful stories. We can&nbsp;
do all the things, we can write all the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:36.880 --> 02:42:42.960
down and look at all the numbers, but we have&nbsp;
to show we care. Because when our non-executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:42.960 --> 02:42:48.800
director and our board colleagues can go out&nbsp;
into services, can speak with women, and show&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:48.800 --> 02:42:52.800
them that they care and show them that they&nbsp;
have the passion for the services, that’s when&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:52.800 --> 02:42:57.600
they open up and start sharing. And that’s when&nbsp;
we’ve got the ability to do the transformation.&nbsp;

02:42:58.160 --> 02:43:03.200
If things are seen as a, you know, a back and&nbsp;
forth, what’s this data say? What’s this? What’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:03.200 --> 02:43:09.040
that? That’s when people clam up. And we had a&nbsp;
long discussion about the care and compassion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:09.040 --> 02:43:14.479
That’s what we’re asking for from our staff. What&nbsp;
we think is safe and what women think is safe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:14.479 --> 02:43:20.080
clearly are very different things. They need to&nbsp;
feel psychologically safe as well as physically&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:20.080 --> 02:43:24.800
safe. We have to embed that culture. And I&nbsp;
think that threads through not just maternity&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:24.800 --> 02:43:30.319
but everything that we do. And that’s another&nbsp;
theme really of the leadership development work&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:30.319 --> 02:43:34.559
that we are doing. We want to work with people&nbsp;
who are passionate about maternity services.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:34.560 --> 02:43:40.319
This isn’t a box to tick. This is the start of,&nbsp;
you know, the next generation. And if we are ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:40.319 --> 02:43:45.040
going to break into health inequalities,&nbsp;
we have to start in maternity services.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:45.040 --> 02:43:50.399
If we don’t get it right in maternity, none of our&nbsp;
other services really stand a very good chance.&nbsp;

02:43:50.399 --> 02:43:54.399
So we have to get a lot more passionate.&nbsp;
Anything that the NHS England and Improvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:54.399 --> 02:43:58.719
boards can do to support the maternity&nbsp;
transformation board to transmit that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:58.720 --> 02:44:04.080
passion on would be always gratefully received.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Sarah-Jane, I’m going to come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:04.080 --> 02:44:09.439
Simon in just a minute. But I remember, and David&nbsp;
Behan will remember this when we both went up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:09.439 --> 02:44:15.759
Morecambe Bay, to meet James Titcombe and some of&nbsp;
the others who’d lost their children in Morecambe&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:15.760 --> 02:44:21.920
Bay, it is a very, very profound and moving&nbsp;
experience to meet mothers who’ve lost their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:22.640 --> 02:44:29.760
babies or husbands who have lost their wives.
So I think Sarah-Jane, perhaps we might arrange&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:29.760 --> 02:44:35.520
a visit and come and sort of see you in Birmingham&nbsp;
at your hospital or maybe go to Shrewsbury. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:36.479 --> 02:44:41.200
you could, maybe Ruth and Sarah-Jane, you could&nbsp;
put your heads together, because I think it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:41.920 --> 02:44:46.319
like Wol says, it does leave&nbsp;
one feeling very sort of uneasy.&nbsp;

02:44:47.520 --> 02:44:53.040
So Simon, you’re going to have to finish I think.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yes. Thank you, David. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:53.040 --> 02:44:55.760
thank you, everybody. I think it’s&nbsp;
been a very powerful discussion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:56.399 --> 02:45:00.879
I think it’s worth drawing out the distinction&nbsp;
which has been implicit in this conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:01.520 --> 02:45:07.920
between the majority of maternity services&nbsp;
which are good and improving, and Sarah-Jane&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:07.920 --> 02:45:15.120
set out some of the data to underpin that&nbsp;
assertion, versus as Aidan has just pointed out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:15.920 --> 02:45:21.279
that smaller number, those smaller number of&nbsp;
units that are isolated, the tail of concern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:21.279 --> 02:45:26.479
where frankly we see unacceptable practice.
And you know, we can call it culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:27.040 --> 02:45:31.279
you can describe it as isolation, but&nbsp;
the fact is that we’ve seen through&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:32.000 --> 02:45:36.319
Morecambe Bay, we’ve seen through East Kent, we’ve&nbsp;
seen through Shrewsbury and Telford, sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:37.279 --> 02:45:44.240
unacceptable standoffs, really, between&nbsp;
different groups of midwives and obstetricians.&nbsp;

02:45:44.240 --> 02:45:49.120
And we’ve just got to, I think, you know, call&nbsp;
it as it is and say that is not good enough, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:49.120 --> 02:45:55.279
we will not put up with that any more.
So I would suggest a couple of next steps&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:55.279 --> 02:45:58.800
on the back of the discussion we’ve just had.&nbsp;
The first is I think it would be very good for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:58.800 --> 02:46:04.000
the boards to hear from Bill Kirkup at some&nbsp;
point over the next six months as to where&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:04.000 --> 02:46:11.439
his work has got to. I commissioned him to do&nbsp;
an external review of services in East Kent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:12.000 --> 02:46:17.680
in the same way that Donna Ockenden has been&nbsp;
doing in Shrewsbury and Telford. And secondly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:17.680 --> 02:46:24.160
I think on the broader change programme, the&nbsp;
96 million pounds that we are allocating today&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:24.160 --> 02:46:28.080
will drive a lot of positive&nbsp;
change across maternity services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:28.080 --> 02:46:32.880
but we should be under no illusion that that will&nbsp;
be mission accomplished. I think this is the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:33.439 --> 02:46:38.879
96 million, we will need subsequent tranches&nbsp;
of additional funding to do the job that needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:38.880 --> 02:46:44.399
doing in maternity services across the NHS.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:44.399 --> 02:46:50.639
Simon. So Ruth, thank you very much. Sarah-Jane,&nbsp;
Aidan, Simon, Simon Eccles, thank you all very&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:50.640 --> 02:46:58.479
much for coming today. And we will definitely&nbsp;
be in touch again for a follow up meeting soon.&nbsp;

02:46:58.479 --> 02:47:03.439
I think getting Bill Kirkup along will be&nbsp;
great. He did the Morecambe Bay review as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:03.439 --> 02:47:09.679
of course. So thank you very much, Ruth.
Right. I think that is the last item&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:09.680 --> 02:47:14.880
on the agenda. There are a few other papers that&nbsp;
are there for noting. Does anyone on the board&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:14.880 --> 02:47:20.640
have any issue or any other business they’d&nbsp;
like to raise or can we conclude the meeting?&nbsp;

02:47:22.720 --> 02:47:30.640
Excellent. Thank you. Thank&nbsp;
you all very much. Thank you.

